WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:13.935
General Gnaeus Julius Agricola stood on the
wet heather at the very edge of humanity.

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:20.060
It was beginning to rain and a misty drizzle
was being carried inland on the saline air.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:25.260
This truly was a savage place - a place where
the gods had not worked their benevolence

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.434
as they had on his Mediterranean home.

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:32.899
He looked out across the deep waters of the outer edge of the known world.

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:38.480
The campaign was over, but Agricola had one
last command to undertake.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.750
After four decades of sporadic conquest and
numerous setbacks, the Roman army was poised

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.190
to finally enter the Highlands of northern
Caledonia, to wipe out

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.340
the remaining native resistance and determine
the limits of Europe.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.900
Agricola would go on to confirm that the British
landmass was an island by navigating unfamiliar

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:04.935
seas, exploring undiscovered coastlines and
making contact with previously unknown peoples.

00:01:04.935 --> 00:01:10.049
Calgacus, the leader of the local barbarians,
the one they called ‘The Swordsman’, had

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:14.260
said that beyond these shores lay only rocks
and sea.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:19.840
That was probably true, but as he watched
the rain clouds roll back over the north-western

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.376
sunset, Agricola wondered…

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:34.251
He cast his mind back thirty years to when Claudius had just begun his conquest of Britain.

00:01:34.251 --> 00:01:39.799
Agricola had been a student in Massalia, ancient Marseille, a Greek-founded city.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.740
Under Roman rule it was a respected centre
of learning with a prominent statue of the

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.990
legendary Greek seafarer Pytheas.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:52.149
Four centuries earlier, in the time of Alexander
the Great, Pytheas had entered the Atlantic

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:57.090
and explored the furthest limits of the ocean
beyond Britain.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:01.840
He had claimed to have seen a further great
landmass positioned far to the north.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.580
It was his belief that three vast islands
formed the North Atlantic Isles – Britain,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.443
Ireland, and Thule.

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.599
“Pytheas states that he set foot on every

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:15.610
part of Britain.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.770
He also gives an account of Thule and describes
a region where he says the land, sea and air

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.300
are no longer separate elements.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.610
Instead, there is a thick compound resembling
a sea-lung (jellyfish) which suspends itself

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.630
over the ocean and land merging and binding
elemental matter.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:41.790
This material cannot be travelled over, or
sailed through.”

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:49.710
In the long half-light before the first stars
appeared, Agricola looked out at the darkening

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:50.720
horizon.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.340
Was the legendary island of Ultima Thule out
there at the very rim of the globe where daylight

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.340
lingered?

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:08.570
And so he mused to himself…if he had come
this far … why not go further?

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:14.069
Beneath him the Roman fleet had reassembled
in a deep inlet to shelter from a sudden autumn

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:15.069
storm.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:22.750
The war galleys and support vessels were making
ready to range northward, supporting the Legions

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.010
who were pushing forward into the Highlands
and forests.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:30.599
Agricola watched as the ships shifted on an
incoming tide.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:36.174
He would have told his fleet commander what to expect out there...

00:03:36.174 --> 00:03:40.632
Roman knowledge of the North sea suggested that latitudes beyond Britain

00:03:40.632 --> 00:03:46.694
would be subject to frozen currents and biting cold winds from the far north.

00:03:46.694 --> 00:03:50.660
And beyond Britain the sea became cold and began to solidify.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:55.500
This process began with platelets of floating
sea-ice that resembled a shoal of encroaching

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.500
jellyfish.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:02.030
But as the cold increased, these ice plates
merged into sheets that could immobilise,

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.180
then crush timber vessels.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.689
Agricola had seen with his own eyes
the white mass of a Caledonian sea mist hang

00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:12.190
over an early frost.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:17.709
Truly a ‘compound resembling a sea-lung’,
as Pytheus had claimed some four centuries

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.489
prior.

00:04:19.489 --> 00:04:25.401
And so - were the Roman military about to
encounter Ultima Thule?

00:04:25.401 --> 00:04:31.720
Would the explorers sent out by Agricola soon
tread the beaches of this remote, mysterious,

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.795
and semi-mythical land?

00:04:34.795 --> 00:04:43.957
But of course this was not the first time the Romans had explored beyond the limits of the known world or crossed the boundaries of knowledge.

00:04:43.957 --> 00:04:49.110
A long sequence of adventure, conquest and
ambition had led Rome and its armies far beyond

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:54.020
the familiar, to the boundaries of the unknown.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.979
Centuries of expansion had pushed the Eternal
city to the ends of the earth - not only to

00:04:59.979 --> 00:05:04.669
the far north, but far east, west and south
too.

00:05:04.669 --> 00:05:09.600
And so this is that story.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:17.296
This is the story of the Edges of the Empire.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.870
The Roman soldier's diet was mainly wheat
- some sources tell us that salt was so prized

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:36.150
that soldiers in the Republic were paid in
salt - and this was the root of the word salary.

