00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:52.389
In the third century BC, the embattled Roman
State was largely confined to Italy with a

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:55.300
powerful network of Italian city-state allies.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:01.250
But in the century that followed, proud legions
captured and subdued Macedonia, seized the

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:06.900
prosperous island of Sicily and laid claim to territories
in western Spain.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.520
Finally, after generations of persistent rivalry
and warfare they closed in on the already

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:17.360
beaten and beleaguered Carthaginians on the
coast of North Africa.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.879
Following a three-year siege, the monumental
conflict between these two opposing empires

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:25.360
was at last concluded.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.979
The Roman general Scipio Aemilianus oversaw
the final brutal capture and destruction of

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:32.949
the seaport of Carthage.

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:37.810
He gave orders that the Roman army should
burn, destroy and demolish everything in their

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:38.810
path.

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:44.550
During six days of savage fighting in the
city streets the order was repeatedly given

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.220
to ‘clear the thoroughfares’.

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:51.940
The wounded and dying citizens were dragged
away and heaped along with corpses and debris

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.470
from collapsing buildings.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:59.970
Then both living and dead bodies were cast
into pits filled with rubble and other charred

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.110
remains.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:04.150
Appian writes:
To make the streets passable they cleared

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.560
the debris with axes, mattocks, and pronged
tools.

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.169
With these iron instruments the dead and the
living were rolled and dragged along like

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:16.010
debris before being hurled together into pits.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:19.340
The trenches were filled with the dead and
still living.

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:25.770
Finally, over 50,000 traumatised survivors,
who had taken refuge in an inner citadel,

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:26.770
surrendered.

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:33.330
They were secured in the iron chains of the
Roman slave dealers.[1]

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:38.640
Surrounded by his closest advisors, Scipio
oversaw this final destruction of the city

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.760
from a distant vantage point.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:46.640
As the ruins of Carthage smouldered down to
embers and ashes, Scipio is said to have suddenly

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.250
stood very still in quiet reflection.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.560
The battle was over.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.470
The threat of death was gone and the thrill
of killing had subsided.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.260
He became lost in thought.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.660
Was this the ultimate destiny of all empires?

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:10.170
Appian records:
Scipio beheld the final destruction of a city

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.800
which had flourished 700 years from its foundation.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.290
It had ruled over so many lands, islands,
and seas.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:22.269
It had been prosperous with great armies and
fleets, elephants, and wealth of money.

00:09:22.269 --> 00:09:27.600
It had been equal to the mightiest monarchies,
but surpassed them all in bravery and ambition

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:34.000
[…] Now the city had met its end in a final
and complete destruction.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:40.040
Scipio, beholding this spectacle, is said
to have shed tears and publicly lamented the

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.330
destiny of his enemy.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:48.709
A Greek advisor called Polybius, who was standing
nearby, overheard Scipio mutter a quote from

00:09:48.709 --> 00:09:55.170
Homer - a verse from the Iliad concerning
the destruction of the legendary city of Troy.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:10:00.029
The great campaign was over, but what next?

00:10:00.029 --> 00:10:06.390
Perhaps, thought Polybius - a new Odyssey
was about to begin.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:13.339
He turned his back on the broken city and
looked out towards the sea where new possibilities

00:10:13.339 --> 00:10:15.209
awaited.

00:10:15.209 --> 00:10:19.829
Could he become a new Odysseus?

00:10:19.829 --> 00:10:24.380
Rome was victorious, Carthage was gone, and
its possessions in North Africa were now the

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.070
property of the expanding Roman Empire.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:31.779
The sea-lanes leading into the Atlantic were
suddenly open to Roman shipping, but what

00:10:31.779 --> 00:10:37.890
unknown lands lay beyond the Mediterranean
and the fabled Pillars of Hercules?

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:44.170
Scipio was determined to destroy all remnants
of Carthage and this included its diaspora.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:48.510
The Carthaginians had established small colonies
and trade stations on the arid outer shores

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.550
of North Africa (modern-day Morocco).

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:55.639
Had these Atlantic connections provided the
Carthaginians with their great wealth and

00:10:55.639 --> 00:10:56.880
good fortune?

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:57.880
Perhaps.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:03.720
So Scipio offered his Greek advisor Polybius
command of several Roman war-galleys and instructed

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:10.010
him to find the westward limits of Carthaginian
power.

00:11:10.010 --> 00:11:14.110
And so his journey west began.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:18.959
By following the African coast, Polybius passed
the High Atlas Mountain range and prepared

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:20.790
to enter the Atlantic.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:24.510
But he was crossing through more than a physical
limitation.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:30.100
He was intruding into the domain of gods,
myths, heroes, and monsters.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:35.740
Deities always existed, where practical experience
faltered, and common knowledge ended.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:42.380
As they passed into uncharted regions, Polybius
and his small fleet, like Odysseus, prepared

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:50.329
themselves for supernatural hazards as well
as physical dangers.

00:11:50.329 --> 00:11:59.200
To begin with, it was said that the world-bearing
Titan Atlas held the weight of the sky on

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.200
his shoulders.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:05.209
Indeed, the fossilised remains of this godlike
giant might yet be seen in the High Atlas

00:12:05.209 --> 00:12:06.209
Mountains.

00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:10.829
Perhaps his massive form would be glimpsed
as a vast mountain column projecting upwards,

00:12:10.829 --> 00:12:13.250
far beyond the tallest peaks.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:19.070
The Romans strained their eyes looking inland
at the ranges, to discern the shape or form

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.630
of the primordial Titan.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:26.660
At the edge of the Mediterranean a high cliff-like
promontory hove into view on the European

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:27.660
coast.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:33.250
This was the Rock of Gibraltar which formed
one flank of the legendary ‘Pillars of Hercules’,

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.970
the gateway between the Mediterranean and
the Atlantic.

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:42.170
It was said that the legendary demi-god Hercules
had cut a channel through a mountain to join

00:12:42.170 --> 00:12:47.050
the seas and enter the Atlantic - though other
accounts suggested Hercules had merely fortified

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:52.820
an existing sea-passage, so that giant oceanic
monsters might not stray into the peaceful

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.210
Mediterranean.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:13:02.410
This rock hewn passage was the strait through
which Polybius and his ships now passed.

00:13:07.064 --> 00:13:12.490
As he passed this legendary spot, Polybius
must have considered the reports of his Greek

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:17.810
forebears, who had themselves heard second-hand
accounts from Carthaginian seafarers.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:24.310
For it had been in the sixth century BC that
a Carthaginian commander named ‘Hanno the

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:29.480
Navigator’ had voyaged beyond the Pillars
of Hercules and explored the western seaboard

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:31.600
of the African continent.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.550
Hanno had left an account of his voyage - this
had been translated into Greek and circulated

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:40.050
among Roman authorities in an abbreviated
form.

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:45.800
It was said that Hanno had commanded a settler
fleet intended to colonise distant regions

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:51.779
- but south of Morocco the Carthaginians had
encountered only a bleak and hostile coastline.

00:13:51.779 --> 00:13:56.030
The Greek writer Arrian read the translated
report:

00:13:56.030 --> 00:14:02.170
“...as Hanno sailed south, he encountered
crippling obstacles – a lack of water, scorching

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:07.350
heat and streams of lava gushing into the
sea.

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:12.110
Perhaps these explorers had sailed beyond
the southern limit of the vast Saharan Desert

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:18.450
and witnessed volcanic lava spilling into
the sea at Mount Cameroon.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:22.330
More terrifying were the human-like creatures
encountered in the region.

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:27.149
Hanno had reported:
In this lagoon was another island, full of

00:14:27.149 --> 00:14:28.149
savages.

00:14:28.149 --> 00:14:31.900
Most of them were women with hairy bodies,
whom our interpreters called ‘Gorillas’.

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:35.720
Although we chased them, we could not catch
any males.

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:40.600
They all escaped, since they were good climbers
and could defended themselves with stones.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.149
However, we caught three women.

00:14:42.149 --> 00:14:46.740
They refused to follow those who carried them
off, biting and clawing at them.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:50.650
So we killed them and flayed them and brought
their skins back to Carthage.

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:57.360
We could not sail any further, because our
provisions were running short.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:02.769
These pelt-like skins had been taken to Carthage
and hung as curiosities in the Temple of the

00:15:02.769 --> 00:15:09.130
Goddess Tanit - macabre trophies lingering
on as a testament to a strange, never to be

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:14.540
repeated voyage.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:20.459
For almost four centuries the hides hung silently
in the shrine, until the destruction of Carthage

00:15:20.459 --> 00:15:24.690
when the Romans looted the city and burned
the sacred temple.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:30.510
By contrast, the temperate shores of western
Europe were explored by Carthaginian merchants

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:35.980
who sailed to distant northern islands in
pursuit of valuable resources.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:40.310
Somewhere out in the Atlantic Ocean were trade
routes to the distant Isle of Albion (Britain)

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:41.420
and Ierni (Ireland).

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:46.519
The Celtic peoples of southern Britain supplied
essential tin which was alloyed with commonly

00:15:46.519 --> 00:15:50.230
found copper to produce sturdy, alluring bronze
metal.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:54.819
Other strange materials reached the Mediterranean
through these Atlantic sea-lanes including

00:15:54.819 --> 00:15:57.190
valuable Baltic amber.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:16:00.930
This was thought to be the congealed rays
of the setting sun.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:06.440
Sometimes strange tropical insects could be
seen in the solidified centre of this highly

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:08.339
desirable substance.

00:16:08.339 --> 00:16:15.459
Perhaps they were evidence of an undiscovered
tropical zone in the faraway frozen north?

00:16:15.459 --> 00:16:21.440
And so it was, that after the destruction
of Carthage, Scipio sent out a small exploratory

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.610
Roman fleet commanded by Polybius.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:29.240
Passing cautiously through the Pillars of
Hercules, they sailed south around the coast

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:30.610
of Morocco.

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:36.110
The Roman writer Pliny the Elder records:
After sailing round the coast, Polybius reported

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:41.770
that, in a westerly direction beyond Mount
Atlas, there are forests teeming with the

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:43.690
wild animals that Africa produces…

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:48.660
Beyond this is the river Non, which is full
of crocodiles and hippopotami.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:53.540
From the Non there is a line of mountains
going right up to a high peak that the Greeks

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:58.550
call ‘The Chariot of the Gods’.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:03.959
With their greatest rival, Carthage, removed
from power, the Romans had finally reached

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:09.079
the western gateway of the Mediterranean and
entered the Atlantic Ocean.

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:12.010
The coastal ports of western Gaul now within
their reach.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:18.400
A new direction for exploration, contact and
conquest had been revealed.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:27.519
The routes of Roman fortune were now opened
into the west - but this was just the beginning.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:52.720
In another region of the ancient world, another
leading general of the Roman Republic was

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:58.919
also suddenly overcome by emotion at his very
moment of supreme victory.

00:17:58.919 --> 00:18:06.230
Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus, Pompey the Great,
stood in shock as he was presented with the

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:10.940
semi-embalmed corpse of his royal enemy Mithridates.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:16.020
Those given the task of preserving the royal
remains had failed to remove the brain tissue

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:21.669
and the putrid object set before him filled
Pompey with a deep revulsion.

00:18:21.669 --> 00:18:27.669
Nevertheless, one of the greatest foes the
Romans have ever faced - was dead.

00:18:27.669 --> 00:18:39.430
Pompey’s grand campaign for vengeance, exploration
and conquest . was over.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:45.550
The year was 65 BC and yet another great kingdom
had fallen to the rising power of Rome and

00:18:45.550 --> 00:18:47.850
its relentless legions.

00:18:47.850 --> 00:18:52.660
The Pontic King Mithradates VI, Eupator had
been betrayed by his allies and subjects in

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:56.200
the Crimean Kingdom of the Cimmerian Bosporus.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:19:00.770
This had been his last refuge, and the king
had ended his own life rather than suffer

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:04.159
humiliation as a captive in Rome.

00:19:04.159 --> 00:19:09.530
In his pursuit of Mithridates, Pompey the
Great had entered the Caucasus Mountains,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:14.640
passed through the dark forests of Colchis
and explored the snow-capped mountains between

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:19.220
the turbulent Black Sea and the mysterious
Caspian region.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:24.920
Now that the archenemy of Rome was dead, the
General reflected on the extraordinary discoveries

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:28.480
he had made in these far eastern territories.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:33.440
The heavily forested territory of Colchis,
on the west coast of the Black Sea, had always

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:39.230
been a dangerous and largely unknown region
- a wilderness zone where tall dense forests

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:41.610
fringed mountain ranges.

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:46.230
Near where the Rioni river emptied into the
Black Sea was an ancient Greek harbour-town

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:47.409
named Phasis.

00:19:47.409 --> 00:19:53.660
A frontier trading outpost that retained its
wild and dangerous character throughout antiquity,

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:59.510
statues of classical gods stood in the harbour
and town centre displaying the strange influence

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:02.150
of barbaric local cults.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:08.409
The site was a hub for numerous merchants
and fugitives who mingled with guides, translators

00:20:08.409 --> 00:20:12.580
and dubious representatives from the nearby
mountain tribes.

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:19.010
And so it was Phasis that had served as the
outer limit of the furthest Greek voyages.

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:23.690
Indeed, not even Alexander the Great had dared
to enter this region.

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:29.650
This meant Colchis was the subject of longstanding
myth and legend - once again a region thought

00:20:29.650 --> 00:20:33.380
to encroach on the realm of the gods.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:39.240
Many in Rome must have wondered - what had
Pompey found out there - at the limits of

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:46.440
the known world?

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:51.100
Greek myth had long warned of the dangers
of exceeding human knowledge contrary to the

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:53.210
will of the Gods.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:58.480
It was said that the Titan Prometheus had
fashioned mankind from clay, giving his creation

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:00.419
thought and reason.

00:21:00.419 --> 00:21:04.929
But this primaeval deity had suffered eternal
punishment for giving his prodigies the key

00:21:04.929 --> 00:21:09.390
to technology - an understanding of fire.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:15.080
On the orders of Zeus, Prometheus had supposedly
been chained to a rock in the furthest reaches

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:17.650
of the Caucasus Mountains.

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:22.780
Bound on a high mountain peak, a giant eagle
would descend each day to tear out the innards

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:26.440
of the helpless immortal.

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:31.659
Ancient Colchis was too immortalized in the
story of Jason and the Argonauts - who sought

00:21:31.659 --> 00:21:35.890
the Golden Fleece in this forbidding region.

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:41.280
Accounts of gold dust and giant dragon-serpents
filled the minds of Greek writers regarding

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.640
these unexplored mountains.

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:48.750
The main anchor from Jason’s ship the Argo
was on display in the centre of Phasis as

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:51.270
a monument to the ancient myth.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:55.150
Some visitors doubted the authenticity of
this object since it was made of relatively

00:21:55.150 --> 00:22:00.630
un-weathered iron, but there sceptics were
shown fragments of an older stone object that

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:04.870
better fitted the antiquity of the myth.

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:10.870
And so this was the place where King Mithridates
had fled to rally his tribal allies against

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:22.830
Rome, and this was where Pompey had to follow
to defeat this great enemy.

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:27.419
But Pompey was also eager to lead his soldiers
into a land of myth and legend.

00:22:27.419 --> 00:22:31.200
According to Appian:
Pompey wanted to gain knowledge of the country

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:35.830
visited by the Argonauts, Castor and Pollux,
and Heracles.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:40.890
He especially desired to see the place where
they say that Prometheus was fastened to Mount

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:42.670
Caucasus.

00:22:42.670 --> 00:22:47.650
From the Pontic Kingdom, Pompey marched on
and invaded the small kingdom of Caucasian-Iberia.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:54.111
Colchis was now cut-off from Armenia and Iran,
but Pompey did not push onwards to the coast

00:22:54.111 --> 00:22:56.320
of the mysterious Caspian Sea.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:05.280
Instead, he headed inland to find the Rioni
River and capture the city-port of Phasis.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:09.039
This connected him with the Roman fleet operating
in the Black Sea.

00:23:09.039 --> 00:23:13.659
But once more he discovered that Mithridates
had fled.

00:23:13.659 --> 00:23:18.400
Pompey thought of pursuing the fugitive with
the Roman fleet and sailing past Mount Strobios

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:20.520
where Prometheus had been chained by Zeus.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:26.150
Would a Roman general dare look upon the site
where the creator of mankind had withstood

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:28.740
his eternal punishment?

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:32.860
But suddenly, a new plan entered the mind
of Pompey.

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:38.539
He realised that he would only retain his
command while Mithridates remained a threat

00:23:38.539 --> 00:23:40.460
to Rome.

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:47.240
While the defeated king was confined to the
far north, the Near East was exposed to immediate

00:23:47.240 --> 00:23:52.220
Roman conquest.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:56.730
Plutarch reports:
Pompey was seized by a great and eager passion

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:01.100
to claim Syria and march through Arabia to
the Red Sea.

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:05.850
His victorious career would therefore touch
the Ocean which surrounds the world on all

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:06.850
sides.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:11.840
For in Africa he had been the first to conquer
as far as the Outer Sea and in Spain he had

00:24:11.840 --> 00:24:15.100
made the Atlantic Ocean the western boundary
of the Roman domains.

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:16.890
In his recent pursuit of the Albani, he had
narrowly missed reaching the Caspian Sea.

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:23.250
So, now Pompey put his army in motion, determined
to reach the Red Sea and complete the circuit

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:25.450
of his military expeditions.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:31.590
Pompeii marched south with his legions, seizing
Syria and making the kingdom of Judea subject

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:32.830
to the Roman Empire.

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:38.390
In Jerusalem, Pompeii and his entourage entered
the Great Temple of the ‘One True God’

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:39.390
of the Jews.

00:24:39.390 --> 00:24:44.789
They ignored all appeals and threats by the
priests and defied the Holy Law by gazing

00:24:44.789 --> 00:24:46.780
upon the artefacts of the Lord.

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:52.610
As his men held back the protesting High Priest,
Pompeii inhaled the heavy scent of hallowed

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:58.221
incense and stepped forward, to vanish behind
the veil that covered the darkness of the

00:24:58.221 --> 00:24:59.909
inner sanctuary.

00:24:59.909 --> 00:25:04.500
His soldiers waited for the general to reappear
with the idol of the ‘Lord God Almighty’

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:05.500
of the Jews.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:11.730
Would he smash it before the distraught priests,
or carry it back to be mocked in a great triumphal

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:15.060
procession through the streets of Rome?

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:19.279
Time passed, --- but he did not immediately
reappear.

00:25:19.279 --> 00:25:25.880
Josephus records at Pompey had simply examined
the artefacts preserved in that holy place,

00:25:25.880 --> 00:25:27.770
but removed nothing.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:37.860
He reappeared empty handed - - - and then
departed in silence.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:41.580
But Pompey continued the march south.

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:45.860
The Nabatean Kingdom in northwest Arabia was
next to submit to Rome and the legions were

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.990
encamped near the rock-cut city of Petra.

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:55.780
All the threats of divine curses and catastrophes
had proven false and the Near East was now

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:58.850
under imperial control.

00:25:58.850 --> 00:26:03.650
The region belonged to Rome and the outer
sea was within their grasp.

00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:08.880
But the campaign led by Pompey had far exceeded
its objectives.

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:13.630
This was irrefutable when news reached him
that Mithridates was already dead.

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:18.200
There had been an uprising in the Cimmerian
Bosporus and the king had ended his life as

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:21.970
a final escape from the pursuits of the Romans.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:25.500
The war was done.

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:30.860
Pompey received the messengers and was presented
with the semi-embalmed corpse of Mithridates.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:35.750
But the general chose not to look upon the
putrid remains or reflect upon this man’s

00:26:35.750 --> 00:26:42.539
great fall from regal power and glory - for
it was now he realised that the exploratory

00:26:42.539 --> 00:26:47.640
aspect of his mission must also cease.

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:52.789
Pompey had failed to encounter any monsters
or meet any living divinities in the distant

00:26:52.789 --> 00:26:53.789
Caucuses.

00:26:53.789 --> 00:26:59.880
But at Phasis, the Romans did find Indian
commodities among the goods trafficked overland

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:01.640
from the Caspian Sea.

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:08.279
These Indian goods came from Bactria (Afghanistan)
via the Oxus River to the Caspian Coasts.

00:27:08.279 --> 00:27:13.580
Pompey had discovered a possible route to
India that could bypass Iran and the rival

00:27:13.580 --> 00:27:15.580
Parthian Empire.

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:21.020
But who amongst ordinary merchants and travellers
would dare traverse this route through distant

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:23.870
and dangerous lands?

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:26.190
Not Pompey.

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:31.850
Fifteen years later he finally met his fate
in the east.

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:37.149
Defeated by Julius Caesar in the Roman Civil
Wars, the dejected general was betrayed by

00:27:37.149 --> 00:27:40.330
his own officers and murdered.

00:27:40.330 --> 00:27:46.750
When his severed head was presented to Caesar,
the dictator too turned away in disgust and

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:48.030
horror.

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:54.289
Despite the political conflict, Caesar mourned
the loss of his former friend and colleague.

00:27:54.289 --> 00:28:00.470
From that moment on, it would be Caesar and
his dynasty who would decide the fate and

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:10.174
further expansion of the Roman Empire.

00:28:24.530 --> 00:28:30.900
Eudaemon Arabia, in ancient Yemen, had been
the main harbour of the mysterious Sabaeans.

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:37.980
But now the harbour-buildings were engulfed
in flames, sending high columns of acrid smoke

00:28:37.980 --> 00:28:40.140
ascending into the sky.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:45.340
For Eudaemon Arabia had been the victim of
a Roman raid designed to test the logistics

00:28:45.340 --> 00:28:51.540
and feasibility of a maritime campaign-route
that could reach outer Arabia.

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:55.980
It was the prelude to war.

00:28:55.980 --> 00:29:00.110
Planned as a precursor to a larger invasion
of southern Arabia led by the Roman commander

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:06.360
Aelius Gallus, the attack was ordered by Augustus,
the successor to Julius Caesar and the first

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:08.780
true Emperor of Rome.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:14.490
But what great prize or knowledge had brought
Roman conquest to this distant region?

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:19.269
The answer could be found in the acrid stench
that was streaming from the harbour and rising

00:29:19.269 --> 00:29:26.399
up into the darkening sky, like some perverse
sacrifice to the gods of war.

00:29:26.399 --> 00:29:28.880
Incense.

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:31.440
Arabian incense was sacred to many ancient
cultures.

00:29:31.440 --> 00:29:36.289
Whether you were Roman, Greek, Jew, or Persian,
when the pungent smoke released from burning

00:29:36.289 --> 00:29:42.130
desiccated tree saps ascended skyward, it
carried holy fragrances towards the heavens.

00:29:42.130 --> 00:29:46.590
Indeed, incense was a much more appealing
offering to the many gods than the odour of

00:29:46.590 --> 00:29:47.919
animal sacrifice.

00:29:47.919 --> 00:29:53.730
But it was not not easy to obtain.

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:58.929
Arabia was a remote land of extreme deserts
and the stunted trees that produced this sap

00:29:58.929 --> 00:30:02.000
grew only on its southern coasts.

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:05.821
The cliffs and promontories of this seaboard
were difficult to approach from the coast

00:30:05.821 --> 00:30:10.940
and the region was seldom entered by outsiders,
and so the hardened drops of sap produced

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:17.289
by lacerating the wiry incense trees was sent
inland through desert trails to reach distant

00:30:17.289 --> 00:30:21.940
trade stations near the Mediterranean or the
Persian Gulf.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:27.539
These Incense Trails crossed a thousand miles
through restricted caravan routes where Arabian

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:32.800
merchants carried their precious loads on
heavily burdened camel teams.

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:38.160
Incense was so valuable that even small pouches
of this alluring substance could only be purchased

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:42.840
using multiple pouches of gold and silver
coins.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:47.150
Early accounts of the Biblical King Solomon
describe how he received an incense caravan

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:48.159
from the Queen of Sheba.

00:30:48.159 --> 00:30:51.830
The ‘Book of Kings’ contains the verse:
And Sheba came to Jerusalem with a very great

00:30:51.830 --> 00:30:57.130
caravan, with camels that bore incense, and
very much gold, and precious stones.

00:30:57.130 --> 00:31:03.529
And when she was come to Solomon, she communed
with him concerning all that was in her heart.

00:31:03.529 --> 00:31:11.030
Indeed, in 332 BC Alexander the Great acquired
vast stocks of Arabian incense when he captured

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:14.690
the Phoenician city of Tyre on the Syrian
seaboard.

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:20.720
A decade later, with his Persian conquests
complete, Alexander had planned to seize southern

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:23.500
Arabia by launching a fleet from the Persian
Gulf.

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:29.600
But his sudden, unexplained death in Babylon
had ended the invasion plans.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:38.039
Three centuries would pass before another
great ruler would attempt the conquest.

00:31:38.039 --> 00:31:46.090
And so it was 25 BC when the Emperor Augustus
planned to surpass Alexander and add Arabia

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:50.769
to the Roman domains.

00:31:50.769 --> 00:31:54.450
Although this was an economic decision as
much as anything else.

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:59.850
Five years earlier, when Egypt became an imperial
province, there had been a boom in consumer

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:05.190
spending across the Empire when vast wealth
was released from the Ptolemaic royal treasuries

00:32:05.190 --> 00:32:08.360
to be awarded to Roman citizens.

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:13.120
Augustus understood that large quantities
of Roman gold and silver were passing into

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:17.639
the hands of Arab merchants through the incense
traffic and trade.

00:32:17.639 --> 00:32:22.950
This was an outpouring of finite imperial
bullion to acquire a renewable resource.

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:28.390
Why then, shouldn’t the Romans possess this
region and control the substance that was

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:30.720
most sacred to their gods?

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:35.750
Strabo reports:
A report had prevailed from early times, that

00:32:35.750 --> 00:32:40.480
the Arabians had become very wealthy from
trading aromatics and valuable gemstones for

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.260
gold and silver.

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:46.360
But they never exchanged any part of this
accumulated wealth with outsiders.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:54.090
Augustus therefore expected to deal with wealthy
allies, - - - or master wealthy enemies.

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:59.740
With this conquest, the Romans could even
dream of outflanking the rival Parthian Empire

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:04.110
which dominated Iran and controlled the Persian
Gulf.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.840
And so - war.

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:12.330
In preparation, the Roman Governor of Egypt,
Aelius Gallus, was given command of 10,000

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:13.890
soldiers, equivalent to two complete legions.

00:33:13.890 --> 00:33:19.039
At first the Romans assumed that a seaborne
campaign conducted through the Red Sea would

00:33:19.039 --> 00:33:21.700
provide the best route to reach the Sabaean
Kingdom.

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:24.600
But the reports were not favourable.

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:30.289
The Red Sea coasts of Arabia were barren and
largely inaccessible due to strong tidal currents

00:33:30.289 --> 00:33:33.151
and dangerous uncharted reefs.

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:41.220
It was therefore decided that the Roman conquest
of Arabia would be a full-scale landward invasion

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:53.271
through the hostile desert along the mysterious
caravan routes.

00:33:53.271 --> 00:33:58.929
Gallus crossed from Egypt into the neighbouring
kingdom of Nabataea in northwest Arabia.

00:33:58.929 --> 00:34:03.840
This territory was still subject to the Empire
and the Roman army was received and resupplied

00:34:03.840 --> 00:34:06.409
at the oasis city of Petra.

00:34:06.409 --> 00:34:11.500
In neighbouring Judea, King Herod the Great
sent 500 desert-equipped soldiers to assist

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:13.409
the Romans on their campaign.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:20.119
Led by Nabatean military guides the Legionaries
marched past the giant rock-cut edifices that

00:34:20.119 --> 00:34:22.390
flanked the main routes into Petra.

00:34:22.390 --> 00:34:28.370
They must have marvelled at the salmon-coloured
cliffs and the massive ornate buildings carved

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:31.329
into these steep rock-facades.

00:34:31.329 --> 00:34:36.960
This was only the outer frontier of the lucrative
Incense Trails that extended vast distances

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:39.490
across the shifting sands.

00:34:39.490 --> 00:34:44.609
Surely there were greater wonders in the as
yet unexplored desert itself?

00:34:44.609 --> 00:34:49.050
The campaign force successfully captured a
series of oasis towns and outposts which became

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.460
a network for future Roman travel.

00:34:51.460 --> 00:34:56.380
But their progress was extremely slow and
after six months on the outbound operations,

00:34:56.380 --> 00:34:57.880
their supplies dwindled.

00:34:57.880 --> 00:35:03.670
A suspicion began to form in the minds of
some soldiers crossing this forbidding terrain.

00:35:03.670 --> 00:35:08.980
What if the Nabateans had contrived some treachery
against the Romans to preserve their own stake

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:13.600
in the lucrative incense trade?

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:21.960
Finally, the Romans did reach the Sabaean
Kingdom on the southern edge of Arabia.

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:28.579
Rocky hills rose into view and groves of palm
trees and cultivated fields became more frequent.

00:35:28.579 --> 00:35:33.200
Great dams had been constructed to collect
streams from the coastal highlands to irrigate

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:35.010
this desert periphery.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:40.540
Crops provided sustenance for cluttered mud-brick
cities encircled by tall walls.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:46.670
On the plains before the settlements, ranks
of disciplined legionaries formed-up to engage

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:52.320
poorly equipped mob-like defenders with little
experience in professional warfare.

00:35:52.320 --> 00:36:00.150
The Sabaean army fled to their cities and
prepared to defend their walled capital Marib.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:03.890
The legions had almost reached their objective.

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:09.760
But as the siege of Marib continued, a mysterious
affliction spread through the troops.

00:36:09.760 --> 00:36:14.500
Their limbs weakened and their teeth began
to loosen in their mouths.

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:18.730
Maybe they were offending the gods by seeking
to possess the incense?

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:22.580
Had the stricken Roman army fallen from divine
favour?

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:27.980
The effects were real and the psychological
impact traumatic.

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:32.780
Roman medics could not diagnose the true cause
of this unknown aliment and they urged Gallus

00:36:32.780 --> 00:36:36.040
to withdraw before the entire army succumbed.

00:36:36.040 --> 00:36:41.170
Perhaps the condition was scurvy caused by
a lack of Vitamin C in the campaign diet - the

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:45.819
Nabatean supply-lines must have been at their
maximum capacity and the Sabaeans would have

00:36:45.819 --> 00:36:51.339
stripped all food resources from the land
as they retreated within their walled cities.

00:36:51.339 --> 00:36:58.569
Finally, the increasingly diminished and demoralised
Roman army began a desperate retreat north

00:36:58.569 --> 00:37:00.760
to Nabatean territory.

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:04.740
Strabo reports:
Gallus was informed by his captives that he

00:37:04.740 --> 00:37:08.540
was only two days' journey from the country
that produces aromatics.

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:16.240
But he had used up six months on his outbound
marches because of bad guidance.

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:22.540
Gallus reached Petra in sixty days, but many
soldiers had died on the journey due to hardships,

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:25.770
disease, and insufficient supplies.

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:30.690
Gallus possibly went on to report that something
in the water of southern Arabia was harmful

00:37:30.690 --> 00:37:33.580
to people from Mediterranean climates.

00:37:33.580 --> 00:37:39.270
And so even though Arabia had been invaded
and explored, Augustus concluded that it could

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:43.180
never be permanently conquered or added to
the Roman domains.

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:51.119
This route to India was closed.

00:38:04.073 --> 00:38:10.310
In AD 52, a group of distinguished royal envoys
from a remote kingdom were ushered into the

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:14.780
audience hall of the Roman Emperor Claudius.

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:19.520
These astonishing strangers, with their dark
complexions, were from the far south.

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:26.040
They were the mysterious ‘Counterlanders’
who lived beneath another sky and witnessed

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:28.950
the influence of different stars.

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:32.950
Pliny records:
The envoys marvelled at the new aspect of

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:35.060
the heavens visible in our country.

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:38.849
They observed the stars that formed the Great
Bear (Ursae Majoris), the Little Bear (Ursa

00:38:38.849 --> 00:38:40.130
Minor) and the Pleiades.

00:38:40.130 --> 00:38:44.590
They told us that in their own country even
the moon only appears above the horizon from

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:47.440
the 8th to the 18th day of the month.

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:52.079
They said that Canopus, a large and brilliant
star, lights their sky by night.

00:38:52.079 --> 00:38:58.260
But what surprised them most was that their
shadows fell towards our sky and not towards

00:38:58.260 --> 00:38:59.260
theirs.

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:05.960
Also, the sun rose on the left-hand side of
the observer and set upon the right.

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:10.300
These ‘Counterlanders’ came from an island
on the outer edge of India, known to Greek

00:39:10.300 --> 00:39:15.710
and Roman merchants as Taprobane – ancient
Sri Lanka.

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:21.650
This was the most distant diplomatic contact
the Roman Empire had never received.

00:39:21.650 --> 00:39:25.270
It seems that a tax-collecting Roman businessman,
a freedman of Annius Ploclamus, had made contact

00:39:25.270 --> 00:39:26.270
with this semi-legendary region.

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:33.640
The result was this remarkable event - the
arrival of distant envoys seeking opportunities

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:39.820
for political friendship and commercial alliance
in Rome.

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:43.910
But this wasn´t the first time the Indian
subcontinent had come into contact with the

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:46.420
Greco-Roman world.

00:39:46.420 --> 00:39:52.490
Half a millennium earlier in 518 BC, the first
Greeks to explore India had arrived as part

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:58.270
of the Persian army commanded by the Achaemenid
King, Darius the Great.

00:39:58.270 --> 00:40:03.319
Herodotus reports:
Darius discovered the greatest part of Asia.

00:40:03.319 --> 00:40:08.530
He observed how crocodiles could be seen in
the Indus River just like the Nile.

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:13.170
Wishing to know how it connected to the sea,
he sent a team of trustworthy men including

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:16.290
Scylax of Caryanda to sail down the river.

00:40:16.290 --> 00:40:20.349
…they sailed westward and after a voyage
of thirty months, they reached a place in

00:40:20.349 --> 00:40:25.200
Egypt [...] Once the voyage was completed,
Darius conquered the Indus and the Persians

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:27.920
made use of the sea in those regions.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:33.660
Two centuries later, Alexander the Great led
his Greco-Macedonian armies towards India

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:37.600
on his own mission of conquest and exploration.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:43.000
In the high mountain passes leading to the
Hindu Kush, his homesick soldiers thought

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:49.050
they detected signs of their own gods and
heroes who travelled to this remote region.

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:54.290
Could the wild vines and creepers growing
near Nysa be evidence that Bacchus, the Greek

00:40:54.290 --> 00:40:58.710
God of revelry and wine, had passed by this
ancient city?

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:04.369
And perhaps the mountain people who fought
with clubs and wore animal skins were followers

00:41:04.369 --> 00:41:06.700
of Hercules?

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:10.520
Alexander launched his own fleet on the Indus
River to support his army on its progress

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:15.090
south toward the ocean that encircled the
whole world.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:21.340
Marvels were seen on the coast including giant
vivid-coloured clams with folded bivalve shells.

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:28.230
Out at sea the humps of blue whales rose and
fell on the ocean foam, suggesting the presence

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.500
of great leviathans in the unknown depths.

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:34.910
Arrian reports:
Large whales live in the outer ocean and there

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:39.080
are fishes much larger than those that exist
in our inland sea.

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:44.580
Nearchus records that when they departed at
daybreak, they saw water being blown upwards

00:41:44.580 --> 00:41:48.829
from the sea as if it was being propelled
by the force of a waterspout.

00:41:48.829 --> 00:41:54.490
The crew were astonished and asked the pilots
of the fleet what might be causing this disturbance.

00:41:54.490 --> 00:42:00.530
They replied that creatures roving about the
ocean will spout up water to a great height.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:05.140
The sailors were so startled by this sight
that the oars fell from their hands.

00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:08.300
But Nearchus encouraged and cheered them onward.

00:42:08.300 --> 00:42:13.410
As he sailed past the galleys, he signalled
to them, to turn their bows towards the whales

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:15.829
as if to give battle.

00:42:15.829 --> 00:42:20.210
Then raising their war-cry they advanced with
rapid strokes of the oars.

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:24.020
With this great sound the sailors regained
their courage.

00:42:24.020 --> 00:42:30.130
Then, as they neared the monsters, they shouted
with all their effort, bugles blared and the

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:31.550
oars pounded the water.

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:36.820
The whales, now visible at the bows of the
ships, were terrified, and dived into the

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:38.390
depths.

00:42:38.390 --> 00:42:44.230
Under the command of Alexander, Greek military
fleets could have explored the outer limits

00:42:44.230 --> 00:42:45.950
of the Indian Ocean.

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:48.460
But this was not to be.

00:42:48.460 --> 00:42:52.920
Plans were made for the conquest of southern
Arabia, but when Alexander died mysteriously

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:59.530
in Babylonia, his empire was divided between
generals who claimed royal titles and established

00:42:59.530 --> 00:43:02.079
regional dynasties.

00:43:02.079 --> 00:43:08.280
The possibility of trade and communication
with far-off India was lost - for nearly two

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:09.460
centuries.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:15.869
And so it wasn't until 118 BC in one of the
kingdoms founded by Alexander´s generals

00:43:15.869 --> 00:43:21.220
- Ptolemaic Egypt - that contact between west
and east was once again made.

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:26.280
The House of Ptolemy had established ports
and harbours on the Red Sea coast and sent

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:31.829
Greek hunting parties south by ship to capture
live African elephants.

00:43:31.829 --> 00:43:37.290
This created a military network with an infrastructure
through the Eastern Desert to ports furnished

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:40.440
for distant voyages.

00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:45.069
By chance one of these royal patrol ships
encountered a strange wreck with a lone survivor

00:43:45.069 --> 00:43:48.220
clinging to its wooden frame.

00:43:48.220 --> 00:43:53.569
Strabo explains:
According to the account, a certain Indian

00:43:53.569 --> 00:43:58.250
was brought to the king by the patrol ships
that sail the gulf next to Arabia.

00:43:58.250 --> 00:44:03.220
They said they had found him alone and half-dead
on a stranded ship…The king placed the Indian

00:44:03.220 --> 00:44:07.700
under the charge of Greek instructors so that
he could learn our language.

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:12.470
When he had learned Greek, he explained that
he had been on a voyage from India.

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:17.619
His ship had lost its course due to a strange
mischance and only after all his companions

00:44:17.619 --> 00:44:22.230
had died from starvation, he had safely reached
Egypt.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:26.150
When his story was doubted, he promised to
act as guide on the journey to India.

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:33.400
A group of men were selected by the king and
Eudoxus became a member of this group.

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:37.950
Eudoxus (of Cyzicus) was a man inclined to
search out and explore the peculiarities of

00:44:37.950 --> 00:44:43.690
new regions and he was well-informed about
nature.

00:44:43.690 --> 00:44:49.710
Departing on his first voyage in 118 BC, Eudoxus
reached the Indus kingdoms to return with

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:54.780
a rich cargo of spices, pearls, and precious
stones.

00:44:54.780 --> 00:44:59.940
From this moment onward, the realms of India
were within reach of the boldest Mediterranean

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:03.690
merchants who made voyages across the distant
ocean.

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:11.369
But the commerce did not reach its full potential
for almost a century, until Rome annexed Egypt

00:45:11.369 --> 00:45:16.080
and Augustus was proclaimed the first Emperor.

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:23.130
At the beginning of the first century AD Roman
ships did not sail beyond the west coast of

00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:28.140
India - the deep hulled merchant vessels were
too large and cumbersome to safely navigate

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:33.650
the line of reefs, swells and shallows that
extends from India to Sri Lanka.

00:45:33.650 --> 00:45:40.359
And so this island, known to the Romans as
Taprobane, was therefore unexplored territory.

00:45:40.359 --> 00:45:45.440
Roman seafarers even feared that Taprobane
might be the outer edge of some vast southern

00:45:45.440 --> 00:45:52.410
continent - the Antipodes as suggested by
Pythagorean theory - vast land masses or separate

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:56.620
otherworlds, occupying the southern hemisphere.

00:45:56.620 --> 00:46:04.470
But these speculations were finally challenged
in AD 52 during the reign of Claudius, with

00:46:04.470 --> 00:46:07.369
the arrival of the Counterlanders.

00:46:07.369 --> 00:46:12.720
Pliny relays the story of why the Counterlander
envoys were sent to Rome, starting with another

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:18.460
Roman trade vessel ship blown off course:
Taprobane, also known as the territory of

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:23.960
the ‘Counterlanders’, was long considered
to be another world […] That is, until Annius

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:29.440
Plocamus had obtained a contract from the
State Treasury to collect Red Sea taxes.

00:46:29.440 --> 00:46:34.450
One of his freedman agents was sailing round
Arabia when he was carried by gale-force winds

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:38.109
from the north and swept beyond the coast
of Carmania.

00:46:38.109 --> 00:46:43.610
After a fortnight they made the harbour of
Hippuri in Taprobane.

00:46:43.610 --> 00:46:48.710
The unfamiliar looking lost Roman tax collector
had been taken to the Sinhalese king and his

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:52.589
extraordinary cargo inspected by royal agents.

00:46:52.589 --> 00:46:56.530
The strange vessel and its prized cargo seemed
astonishing.

00:46:56.530 --> 00:47:01.680
The ship was carrying a large consignment
of high-value imperial gold and silver coinage

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:05.520
that had been destined for the southern Arabian
incense markets.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:10.780
The Sinhalese king and his royal ministers
carefully examined the foreign coins.

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:16.760
They scrutinised the metallic faces of distant
rulers and marvelled at the fine die-struck

00:47:16.760 --> 00:47:20.380
portraits of Augustus, Tiberius, Caligula,
and Claudius.

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:27.670
The reverse of these coins depicted the outline
of strange buildings, foreign temples, and

00:47:27.670 --> 00:47:30.660
the images of unknown gods.

00:47:30.660 --> 00:47:34.410
Within six months the Roman traveller had
learnt the local language and could converse

00:47:34.410 --> 00:47:35.830
with his hosts.

00:47:35.830 --> 00:47:41.559
Could the accounts he told them of his home
in a great western civilisation possibly be

00:47:41.559 --> 00:47:42.559
true?

00:47:42.559 --> 00:47:46.859
Pliny records how the Sinhalese king sent
four envoys to investigate the Roman Empire

00:47:46.859 --> 00:47:53.280
and introduce the Anuradhapura Realm as a
political ally and commercial associate.

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:58.450
One of the final notable acts of the Emperor
Claudius was to receive and acknowledge these

00:47:58.450 --> 00:48:00.850
exotic ambassadors.

00:48:00.850 --> 00:48:06.839
This meant that during his time as emperor,
Claudius had both occupied Britain and established

00:48:06.839 --> 00:48:18.480
diplomatic contacts with Sri Lanka – distant
islands on opposite edges of the earth.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:23.359
After this remarkable contact, Roman ships
began making direct ocean crossings to Sri

00:48:23.359 --> 00:48:26.800
Lanka and the Anuradhapura Kingdom.

00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:32.440
Roman captains developed faster voyages across
the ocean and, by sailing around Sri Lanka,

00:48:32.440 --> 00:48:39.480
they extended their ventures to trade ports
in east India, the Ganges and Burma.

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:46.750
A series of ambitious unnamed merchants, each
prepared to undertake longer and riskier voyages,

00:48:46.750 --> 00:48:53.280
added to the range and scope of Roman trade
contacts across the vast eastern ocean.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:59.589
These commercial explorers headed ever further
east, tracking rumours and distant contacts

00:48:59.589 --> 00:49:06.619
towards the mysterious Seres – the Silk
People at the edge of the known world.

00:49:06.619 --> 00:49:25.670
And so the Romans kept pushing the boundaries
of their knowledge.

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:30.329
Centurions from the Praetorian Guard had trekked
for weeks through the canyon deserts of Nubia

00:49:30.329 --> 00:49:32.880
- south of Egypt.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:40.030
Now, finally they were entering the royal
audience hall of the king of Meroe - the leading

00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:42.990
African kingdom in these distant lands.

00:49:42.990 --> 00:49:48.370
These Roman officers, caked with the debris
and dust of long-distance travel, had journeyed

00:49:48.370 --> 00:49:52.790
more than 900 miles from the frontiers of
Egypt.

00:49:52.790 --> 00:49:57.290
The Empire had already conquered and controlled
the Mediterranean coastline of north Africa

00:49:57.290 --> 00:49:59.160
that faced southern Europe.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:04.059
But this narrow strip was only a fraction
of the vast African continent.

00:50:04.059 --> 00:50:10.290
Inland from the fertile coastal zones, Africa
became increasingly arid as it merged into

00:50:10.290 --> 00:50:16.160
desert wastelands - the Saharan Desert covering
North Africa with a belt of barren land almost

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:22.849
3,000 miles wide, stretching from the Red
Sea to the Atlantic Ocean.

00:50:22.849 --> 00:50:27.480
Extending nearly a thousand miles from north
to south, it had an expanse larger than the

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:30.390
landmass of the entire Roman Empire.

00:50:30.390 --> 00:50:35.790
In the ancient past, Berber caravans with
knowledge of wells and oasis stations were

00:50:35.790 --> 00:50:38.000
able to cross this brutal landscape.

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:44.250
But the extreme conditions confined the Romans
to the seaboard fringe of North Africa.

00:50:44.250 --> 00:50:56.590
Though there was another route across this
desert expanse.

00:50:56.590 --> 00:51:00.850
After the Romans conquered Egypt, they had
gained control of the northern part of the

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:01.869
Nile River.

00:51:01.869 --> 00:51:09.460
But they were reluctant to extend their rule
south into the barren wastes.

00:51:09.460 --> 00:51:21.450
This zone was under the control of an African
kingdom known as Meroe, or ancient Kush.

00:51:21.450 --> 00:51:27.109
The earliest Greek legends recalled an era
when the people of Kush conquered Egypt, and

00:51:27.109 --> 00:51:33.730
African pharaohs with distinctive dark complexions
commanded a unified Nilotic kingdom.

00:51:33.730 --> 00:51:38.760
The Twenty-fifth Dynasty, that had ruled Egypt
during the eighth century BC, was contemporary

00:51:38.760 --> 00:51:43.830
with the famous Greek author Homer who composed
the Iliad and the Odyssey.

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:48.549
Homer had recited the legend of Memnon, the
champion-king of Kush, who led an African

00:51:48.549 --> 00:51:52.830
army north to support the Trojans in their
war against the Greeks.

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:58.930
Memnon, was a warrior of great skill and valour,
but he was slain in epic combat with the supreme

00:51:58.930 --> 00:52:01.790
Greek hero Achilles.

00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:08.210
The Romans regarded the Trojans as their legendary
ancestors - so how should they view Meroe?

00:52:08.210 --> 00:52:12.500
Surely as potential allies?

00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:16.710
Based on Greek knowledge of Africa, the Romans
believed that the Continent terminated just

00:52:16.710 --> 00:52:19.589
south of the vast Saharan Desert.

00:52:19.589 --> 00:52:25.030
Early accounts had confirmed that these distant
territories contained bush savannahs and semi-forested

00:52:25.030 --> 00:52:26.500
regions.

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:30.880
These were the homelands of Africans known
collectively as ‘Ethiopians’.

00:52:30.880 --> 00:52:36.440
Pythagorean theory also suggested that nature
had created symmetry and all natural phenomena

00:52:36.440 --> 00:52:38.750
had an equivalent opposite.

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:43.200
Africa therefore had similar dimensions to
Europe and the Nile was expected to follow

00:52:43.200 --> 00:52:47.650
an east-west direction equivalent to the Danube
River.

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:54.819
But where was its origin?

00:52:54.819 --> 00:52:59.440
Determined to learn the truth of the great
river, the Emperor Augustus sent a Roman army

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:04.609
into the central Sahara led by a commander
named Cornelius Balbus.

00:53:04.609 --> 00:53:11.960
In 20 BC Balbus crossed the inner desert headed
towards the Fezzan region of southern Libya.

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:18.450
He conducted a rapid campaign, marching 600
miles south from Regio Tripolitania on a course

00:53:18.450 --> 00:53:22.829
through the open desert that took at least
twenty-four days to accomplish.

00:53:22.829 --> 00:53:27.510
The Romans captured the main oasis centres
of Garamantia, but their engineers were utterly

00:53:27.510 --> 00:53:33.430
perplexed by the outflow of endless fresh
water the Garamantes had access to.

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:37.859
They reasoned that perhaps the Garamantes
had tapped into a vast underground river.

00:53:37.859 --> 00:53:42.440
Did the Nile actually flow beneath the desert?

00:53:42.440 --> 00:53:47.190
In reality the Garamantes were accessing prehistoric
aquifers.

00:53:47.190 --> 00:53:52.570
These underground water sources were the residue
of vast upland lakes that had seeped below

00:53:52.570 --> 00:53:54.850
the earth in earlier millennia.

00:53:54.850 --> 00:54:00.369
The Romans who surveyed this arid terrain
did not understand the true age of the earth,

00:54:00.369 --> 00:54:05.450
nor could they begin to fathom the extraordinary
alterations caused by changes in climate.

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:11.910
Hence, the underground river theory provided
a credible explanation for hidden water sources.

00:54:11.910 --> 00:54:16.320
Beyond this, all Balbus discovered was desert.

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:22.800
Though he did celebrate a Triumph in Rome,
the Empire withdrew from Garamantic territory.

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:27.780
It seems that Augustus and successors were
prepared to leave the mystery of the Nile

00:54:27.780 --> 00:54:31.740
and its emergence from distant origins unanswered.

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:34.869
But not for long.

00:54:34.869 --> 00:54:40.079
For when the Romans seized direct control
over the subject kingdom of Mauretania in

00:54:40.079 --> 00:54:44.230
AD 40, new discoveries were made.

00:54:44.230 --> 00:54:49.319
Ancient Mauritania was situated on the Mediterranean
seaboard of northwest Africa stretching from

00:54:49.319 --> 00:54:51.780
Algeria to Morocco.

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:57.020
The sudden Roman annexation provoked an uprising
on the desert frontiers.

00:54:57.020 --> 00:55:02.299
Two legionary armies were redeployed to suppress
this rebellion, one of the commanders a man

00:55:02.299 --> 00:55:09.599
named Gaius Suetonius Paulinus - and Gaius
was an ambitious man.

00:55:09.599 --> 00:55:15.830
In AD 41 Paulinus marched his army south towards
the Atlas Mountains that formed the furthest

00:55:15.830 --> 00:55:17.400
limits of the kingdom.

00:55:17.400 --> 00:55:22.299
He led his legionaries through sweltering
forests on the edge of Morocco and encountered

00:55:22.299 --> 00:55:29.289
strange and startling animals including tarantulas,
scorpions, and bizarre monkeys.

00:55:29.289 --> 00:55:34.849
Undeterred, Paulinus ascended and crossed
the Atlas range, passing beneath African peaks

00:55:34.849 --> 00:55:38.730
still covered with snow even during the height
of summer.

00:55:38.730 --> 00:55:44.059
From these high vistas, Paulinus surveyed
the landscape beyond the mountains and saw

00:55:44.059 --> 00:55:48.270
the glint of a river flowing on the arid desert
plains below.

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:54.450
He led his troops down into the scorching
black-sand desert, but when they reached the

00:55:54.450 --> 00:56:02.470
distant river, they discovered it to be a
weak trickle meandering through barren lands.

00:56:02.470 --> 00:56:05.750
Paulinus abandoned his expedition.

00:56:05.750 --> 00:56:13.319
This was the furthest territory that the Romans
had explored, but there was no sign of any

00:56:13.319 --> 00:56:16.839
‘western’ Nile.

00:56:16.839 --> 00:56:20.270
Pliny the Elder hinted at the true purpose
of the mission:

00:56:20.270 --> 00:56:24.039
An exploring party…was recently sent by
the Emperor Nero.

00:56:24.039 --> 00:56:29.170
It seems that, among his other wars, he is
actually contemplating an attack on Ethiopia

00:56:29.170 --> 00:56:31.940
(Inner Africa).

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:38.039
And so, in AD 61, the Praetorians left Roman
territory in Egypt and headed south to seek

00:56:38.039 --> 00:56:40.910
an audience with the king of Meroe.

00:56:40.910 --> 00:56:46.760
Posing as diplomats and explorers, the Roman
party took a careful note of distances and

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:55.990
resources on an estimated 945-mile trek from
Syene to the royal capital of Meroe.

00:56:55.990 --> 00:57:01.270
Ancient Egyptian texts recorded about twenty-five
urban centres along the Nubian Nile.

00:57:01.270 --> 00:57:06.200
However, on their trek south, the Romans discovered
that most of these settlements had been abandoned

00:57:06.200 --> 00:57:11.549
centuries earlier - Praetorians following
the course of the Nile examined dry crumbling

00:57:11.549 --> 00:57:17.940
ruins of outposts and temples built by past
civilisations of immense antiquity.

00:57:17.940 --> 00:57:23.309
Based on these reports, Pliny argued:
It was not Rome that made this country a desert.

00:57:23.309 --> 00:57:28.140
This region was worn out by alternate periods
of dominance and subjection in a series of

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:34.049
wars with Egypt, having been a famous and
powerful country even down to the time of

00:57:34.049 --> 00:57:36.440
the Trojan Wars.

00:57:36.440 --> 00:57:41.309
The Nubian Desert ended near the outskirts
of Meroe.

00:57:41.309 --> 00:57:44.900
Here the land finally became fertile and forested.

00:57:44.900 --> 00:57:48.710
Pliny records:
Greener herbage appears near the city and

00:57:48.710 --> 00:57:54.730
stretches of forest come into view where the
tracks of rhinos and elephants can be seen.

00:57:54.730 --> 00:58:01.440
On arrival at Meroe, the trail-weary Praetorians
declared their status as envoys and asked

00:58:01.440 --> 00:58:05.240
for an audience with the ruler of the city.

00:58:05.240 --> 00:58:09.839
They were eventually taken to the royal audience
chamber.

00:58:09.839 --> 00:58:15.980
In the gloom of the temple-like palace hall,
towering stone statues loomed from between

00:58:15.980 --> 00:58:17.510
ornamental pillars.

00:58:17.510 --> 00:58:24.950
Tall reliefs displayed images of strange lion-headed
gods - but also disturbing scenes of past

00:58:24.950 --> 00:58:25.950
conflicts.

00:58:25.950 --> 00:58:32.329
A century earlier, Merotic armies had stormed
the frontier city of Syene in southern Egypt.

00:58:32.329 --> 00:58:37.570
Taking some Roman prisoners and decapitating
bronze statues of the Emperor Augustus, some

00:58:37.570 --> 00:58:42.880
reliefs in Meroe depicted Roman soldiers made
to kneel in submission.

00:58:42.880 --> 00:58:47.619
It was a reminder that the Empire of Rome
was not always triumphant on the edges of

00:58:47.619 --> 00:58:49.309
the empire.

00:58:49.309 --> 00:58:54.339
The leading tribune of the praetorians stepped
forward to request a favour from the African

00:58:54.339 --> 00:59:01.660
king - a message from the golden Emperor Nero,
the ruler of mighty ‘Arme’ - Imperial

00:59:01.660 --> 00:59:02.750
Rome.

00:59:02.750 --> 00:59:07.160
For Nero wanted something from inner Africa.

00:59:07.160 --> 00:59:11.400
The praetorians waited while the king considered
his response.

00:59:11.400 --> 00:59:16.829
The acrid scent of Somali incense filled the
audience hall, drifting through shafts of

00:59:16.829 --> 00:59:22.369
brilliant sunlight and penetrating through
elevated openings near the ceiling.

00:59:22.369 --> 00:59:28.589
Unfamiliar faces with uniquely dark complexions
scrutinised the new Roman arrivals.

00:59:28.589 --> 00:59:32.670
Exhausted by their travels through strange
and arid lands, the Praetorians must have

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:35.030
struggled to maintain their composure.

00:59:35.030 --> 00:59:42.390
Their fear and unease was justified - Meroe
was not a subject nation and Rome had no dominion

00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:45.319
here.

00:59:45.319 --> 00:59:49.730
But the King of Meroe…offered them support.

00:59:49.730 --> 00:59:53.120
Seneca confirms that:
The King supplied them with assistance and

00:59:53.120 --> 00:59:56.619
gave them letters of introduction to the neighbouring
rulers.

00:59:56.619 --> 01:00:01.809
Then they set out on their long journey into
the centre of Africa.

01:00:01.809 --> 01:00:07.359
The course of the Nile did not extend westward
as most Roman authorities had supposed.

01:00:07.359 --> 01:00:13.339
The Praetorians found themselves pushing deeper
into Africa following the White Nile south

01:00:13.339 --> 01:00:19.309
600 miles until they reached a seemingly impassable
barrier.

01:00:19.309 --> 01:00:20.700
The Sudd.

01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:27.190
This is a primordial wetland in Southern Sudan
filled with ferns, fields of papyrus reeds

01:00:27.190 --> 01:00:29.960
and thick mats of rotting vegetation.

01:00:29.960 --> 01:00:36.069
In the rainy season it becomes a vast humid
swamp teeming with mosquitoes and other insects.

01:00:36.069 --> 01:00:41.069
Those who entered this region had to endure
sweltering heat with the fear of disease and

01:00:41.069 --> 01:00:44.310
the threat of starvation.

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:49.359
The Sudd was too deep to be safely crossed
on foot, yet the water was too shallow to

01:00:49.359 --> 01:00:52.119
be explored any further by boat.

01:00:52.119 --> 01:00:53.880
It was also vast.

01:00:53.880 --> 01:00:58.190
It included almost 300 miles of swollen wetland.

01:00:58.190 --> 01:01:04.520
The heavy clay soils retained and expanded
the outflow of semi-stagnant water, far beyond

01:01:04.520 --> 01:01:07.280
the vision of any explorers.

01:01:07.280 --> 01:01:13.490
The water teemed with strange sharp-finned
fish and the explorers must have kept keen

01:01:13.490 --> 01:01:17.630
watch for the outline of crocodiles among
the drifting debris.

01:01:17.630 --> 01:01:23.890
The dense hulking shapes of hippopotami would
watch, half submerged, snorting in sputtering

01:01:23.890 --> 01:01:26.569
bursts as the travellers passed by.

01:01:26.569 --> 01:01:32.099
They were to be avoided in case these monstrous
animals decided to charge, gore or batter

01:01:32.099 --> 01:01:33.960
crew and craft.

01:01:33.960 --> 01:01:38.410
The Praetorian must have realised that these
beasts were large and powerful enough to overturn

01:01:38.410 --> 01:01:40.840
and smash their small vessels.

01:01:40.840 --> 01:01:44.849
What use were swords and javelins against
such strong and swift moving horrors?

01:01:44.849 --> 01:01:49.859
All the while, the persistent sun blazed down
upon this wretched landscape.

01:01:49.859 --> 01:01:55.849
These sluggish pools teamed with clouds of
incessant mosquitos and even at microscopic

01:01:55.849 --> 01:01:58.569
level this water was a living poison.

01:01:58.569 --> 01:02:04.779
Miniscule water-borne parasites infected the
bladder and kidneys causing skin-irritations,

01:02:04.779 --> 01:02:06.450
stomach cramps and diarrhoea.

01:02:06.450 --> 01:02:12.450
Skin sores, cuts and lesions would not heal
in this infected decaying environment.

01:02:12.450 --> 01:02:17.270
It must have seemed to the despairing Romans
that they had reached the chaotic and primordial

01:02:17.270 --> 01:02:22.990
edge of nature where matter became indistinct,
and creation was half formed.

01:02:22.990 --> 01:02:27.710
Finally, sodden and exhausted the Praetorians
could go no further.

01:02:27.710 --> 01:02:36.869
The Tribune gazed out across the endless shimmering
haze of swamp and gave the order – Turn back

01:02:41.200 --> 01:02:44.369
On their return to Italy, the Roman explorers
told Seneca:

01:02:44.369 --> 01:02:49.369
We came to great swamps, the borders of which
even the natives did not know.

01:02:49.369 --> 01:02:51.760
We could not find the limits of this place.

01:02:51.760 --> 01:02:55.859
The river was completely entangled with vegetable
growth, and the waters were impassable by

01:02:55.859 --> 01:02:57.770
foot because of the muddy overgrowth.

01:02:57.770 --> 01:03:02.829
But we saw two rocks from which an immense
quantity of water issued.

01:03:02.829 --> 01:03:07.779
Perhaps, they prudently suggested, this was
one of the underground sources of the Nile

01:03:07.779 --> 01:03:11.640
issuing forth like a fountain to flood the
land.

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:16.730
From the reports given by the explorers, Roman
cartographers prepared a diagram that outlined

01:03:16.730 --> 01:03:20.880
the main invasion routes into Sub-Saharan
Africa.

01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:26.470
Pliny describes this object as ‘the schematic
plan of Ethiopia recently presented to the

01:03:26.470 --> 01:03:28.540
Emperor Nero’.

01:03:28.540 --> 01:03:31.700
Nero set his plans into action.

01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:36.529
An imperial residence was prepared in the
Egyptian capital Alexandria, and troops were

01:03:36.529 --> 01:03:40.630
sent to the city in preparation for an imminent
campaign.

01:03:40.630 --> 01:03:46.490
Legions X Fretensis and V Macedonica were
sent to Egypt to extend Roman rule deep into

01:03:46.490 --> 01:03:47.510
Africa.

01:03:47.510 --> 01:03:51.329
But it was not to be.

01:03:51.329 --> 01:03:55.790
There was an uprising against the Romans in
Judea at the same time as the legions in northern

01:03:55.790 --> 01:03:58.369
Europe rebelled against the Emperor.

01:03:58.369 --> 01:04:02.750
Eventually, even the Praetorians withdrew
their support and the Senate declared Nero

01:04:02.750 --> 01:04:04.890
an enemy of the state.

01:04:04.890 --> 01:04:09.660
The fugitive emperor fled the imperial palace
and took his own life rather than suffer the

01:04:09.660 --> 01:04:12.319
pain and humiliation of a public execution.

01:04:12.319 --> 01:04:19.660
The Romans would never find the true source
of the Nile - and soon their probing reach

01:04:19.660 --> 01:04:30.772
would turn northwards.

01:04:46.579 --> 01:04:52.089
Much of Northern Europe was covered in a dense
and ancient woodland known to the Romans as

01:04:52.089 --> 01:04:54.369
the Hercynian Forest.

01:04:54.369 --> 01:05:00.380
Tall deciduous trees towered over the thick
leaf-matted forest floor creating a gloomy

01:05:00.380 --> 01:05:06.500
canopy that hemmed in and overshadowed anyone
moving through this vast and claustrophobic

01:05:06.500 --> 01:05:07.950
expanse.

01:05:07.950 --> 01:05:14.440
It was a disorientating wilderness, utterly
strange and disconcerting for Roman travellers.

01:05:14.440 --> 01:05:19.690
Savage wild animals lived in these places
and hostile Germanic tribes-folk could stalk

01:05:19.690 --> 01:05:24.089
or ambush those who trespassed through their
dark domains.

01:05:24.089 --> 01:05:30.980
Scattered somewhere in this silent eerie forest
was the scene of an atrocity – the Teutoburg

01:05:30.980 --> 01:05:33.220
massacre.

01:05:33.220 --> 01:05:38.839
Butchered remains of three entire legions
littered the forest floor, their corpses desecrated

01:05:38.839 --> 01:05:45.000
and displayed on trees in strange Germanic
customs, presented as offerings to savage

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:47.599
hostile gods.

01:05:47.599 --> 01:05:53.329
What could possibly tempt Roman adventurers
to brave this wilderness?

01:05:53.329 --> 01:05:57.849
The answer was amber resin.

01:05:57.849 --> 01:06:02.809
Early Greek theory suggested that amber was
the congealed rays of the sun as it set into

01:06:02.809 --> 01:06:04.079
a frozen atmosphere.

01:06:04.079 --> 01:06:09.650
It had already been established that the cold
of northern regions could turn sea water into

01:06:09.650 --> 01:06:13.550
a solid substance and condense the air into
an impenetrable fog.

01:06:13.550 --> 01:06:18.329
But over time this idea had changed.

01:06:18.329 --> 01:06:23.369
Burning amber chippings produced the smell
of pine sap and there were strange and exotic

01:06:23.369 --> 01:06:29.369
insects, scorpions, beetles, and wasps embedded
in the solidified substance.

01:06:29.369 --> 01:06:33.480
This would suggest that that the material
was probably some sort of tree resin.

01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:37.960
However, the process that transformed sap
to gem was unknown.

01:06:37.960 --> 01:06:40.390
Might the Romans discover the mystery?

01:06:40.390 --> 01:06:45.940
Was there a land of perpetual daylight concealed
in the far north?

01:06:45.940 --> 01:06:55.559
And so Nero´s interest had been piqued.

01:06:55.559 --> 01:07:00.030
Another envoy had returned to Rome having
undertaken a distant mission far beyond the

01:07:00.030 --> 01:07:03.170
frontiers of the Empire for the emperor.

01:07:03.170 --> 01:07:09.010
He had followed trade routes north beyond
the Danube frontiers, crossing 600 miles of

01:07:09.010 --> 01:07:15.329
hostile and unknown territory to acquire a
valuable prize, a mysterious resource from

01:07:15.329 --> 01:07:20.210
the frozen oceans of the far north – Baltic
amber.

01:07:20.210 --> 01:07:26.640
A fossilised tree resin found in deep sub-surface
sediments on the Baltic coast, it was the

01:07:26.640 --> 01:07:33.020
remains of a vast coniferous forest from the
Eocene Epoch over 44 million years ago.

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:39.000
The erosion of this strata by turbulent North
Sea storms and tidal flows cast amber onto

01:07:39.000 --> 01:07:42.349
the shores where it was gathered by ancient
peoples.

01:07:42.349 --> 01:07:47.260
Germanic populations offered amber to the
ancient Britons and conveyed it inland through

01:07:47.260 --> 01:07:52.140
the rivers of central Europe to reach outposts
of classical civilisation in the Black Sea

01:07:52.140 --> 01:07:55.890
and the Mediterranean.

01:07:55.890 --> 01:08:00.730
Centuries before the Roman era, amber from
the Baltic coasts of the North Sea had reached

01:08:00.730 --> 01:08:01.870
the Greek world.

01:08:01.870 --> 01:08:08.400
It was cut, ground and polished into ornaments
and these bright translucent orbs had the

01:08:08.400 --> 01:08:10.369
value of precious gemstones.

01:08:10.369 --> 01:08:16.710
It was a strange and intriguing material used
to adorn Greek necklaces and decorate the

01:08:16.710 --> 01:08:20.210
shields of Carthaginian soldiers.

01:08:20.210 --> 01:08:24.690
Greek myth suggested that pieces of amber
were the tears shed by the sisters of Phaethon

01:08:24.690 --> 01:08:26.830
when they heard of his demise.

01:08:26.830 --> 01:08:31.870
They were the offspring of Helios, the sun
god, who had allowed his child Phaethon, the

01:08:31.870 --> 01:08:35.150
semi-divine youth, to drive the sun chariot.

01:08:35.150 --> 01:08:40.710
But the boy lost control of the speeding vehicle
and tumbled to his death somewhere in the

01:08:40.710 --> 01:08:42.880
frozen north.

01:08:42.880 --> 01:08:47.940
In Roman Syria, amber was prized for the household
craft of spinning yarns.

01:08:47.940 --> 01:08:53.370
It had electrostatic properties and would
draw fibres and threads towards the spindle.

01:08:53.370 --> 01:08:57.359
Amber was therefore known as ‘harpax’
or ‘the snatcher’.

01:08:57.359 --> 01:09:02.069
Pliny observed that when amber was warmed
by rubbing it could ‘pick up dry leaves,

01:09:02.069 --> 01:09:05.659
or straw and catch the fine fringes of garments’.

01:09:05.659 --> 01:09:11.009
Noting these strange properties the Greeks
called amber ‘electrum’ or ‘the shining

01:09:11.009 --> 01:09:12.009
one’.

01:09:12.009 --> 01:09:17.190
Yet, the peoples of northern Europe remained
distant and unknown to the Romans until the

01:09:17.190 --> 01:09:24.549
outbreak of the Cimbric War.

01:09:24.549 --> 01:09:30.819
In about 120 BC, a sudden environmental shift
caused great flood tides around the Jutland

01:09:30.819 --> 01:09:35.590
Peninsula, destroying farmlands, herds and
habitats.

01:09:35.590 --> 01:09:40.779
Faced with starvation, the Germanic peoples
known as the Cimbri and Tuetons began a mass

01:09:40.779 --> 01:09:43.750
migration into Gaul.

01:09:43.750 --> 01:09:48.449
Merging with Celtic warriors and moving with
their families they became an invasion force

01:09:48.449 --> 01:09:51.460
as they pushed south towards Roman Italy.

01:09:51.460 --> 01:09:58.310
In 101 BC after more than a decade of conflict
the Roman Legions finally defeated and destroyed

01:09:58.310 --> 01:10:00.570
the last of these Germanic invaders.

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:06.420
But the distant homelands of these people
remained a curious mystery.

01:10:06.420 --> 01:10:12.890
The Roman annexation of Germania finally began
in 16 BC when the first Emperor Augustus placed

01:10:12.890 --> 01:10:18.690
his adopted son Nero Claudius Drusus in command
of the legions and tasked him with a great

01:10:18.690 --> 01:10:22.280
campaign of conquest and exploration.

01:10:22.280 --> 01:10:26.880
Crossing the Rhine, the armies led by Drusus
reached the Elbe River which flows into the

01:10:26.880 --> 01:10:29.929
North Sea near the Jutland Peninsula.

01:10:29.929 --> 01:10:35.030
When Drusus died from an injury on campaign,
Augustus appointed his successor Tiberius

01:10:35.030 --> 01:10:41.270
as the replacement commander, who launched
seaward operations as far as the Jutland Peninsula

01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:44.510
and secured Greater Germania for the Empire.

01:10:44.510 --> 01:10:48.560
Augustus boasted of these achievements in
his memorial statement:

01:10:48.560 --> 01:10:54.890
I restored the provinces of Gaul and Spain,
and pacified Germany, securing the ocean from

01:10:54.890 --> 01:10:57.179
Cadiz to the mouth of the river Elbe.

01:10:57.179 --> 01:11:02.770
[...] I commanded my ships to sail the ocean
from the mouth of the Rhine east to the lands

01:11:02.770 --> 01:11:07.940
bordering the Cimbri, where no Roman had gone
before either by land or sea.

01:11:07.940 --> 01:11:12.380
The Cimbri, the Charydes, the Semnones and
the other Germans of this territory sent envoys

01:11:12.380 --> 01:11:13.380
seeking alliance with me and the Roman people.

01:11:13.380 --> 01:11:14.980
But the Roman advance would not be a lasting
conquest.

01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:21.310
In AD 9 the Germans staged a rebellion and
massacred three entire Legions in the dark

01:11:21.310 --> 01:11:24.739
and wild Teutoburg Forest.

01:11:24.739 --> 01:11:30.390
The elderly Augustus suffered a severe reaction
when he heard this news, never fully recovering

01:11:30.390 --> 01:11:37.410
- Drusus and Tiberius seemed to have set the
limits for Roman knowledge and expansion on

01:11:37.410 --> 01:11:40.080
the northern fringe of Europe.

01:11:40.080 --> 01:11:45.410
To many Romans it then seemed as if they were
fighting against nature itself in their efforts

01:11:45.410 --> 01:11:48.520
to conquer and pacify Germania.

01:11:48.520 --> 01:11:54.060
The Emperor Tiberius continued the conflict,
but in AD 16 the Roman fleet was caught in

01:11:54.060 --> 01:12:00.410
a severe storm while ferrying a land army
from the Ems River back to the Rhine frontier.

01:12:00.410 --> 01:12:04.210
The disaster was described by a Roman poet
named Albinovanus Pedo.

01:12:04.210 --> 01:12:10.850
The work has not survived, but a passage quoted
by Seneca clearly expresses Roman fears about

01:12:10.850 --> 01:12:12.210
the northern ocean.

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:16.390
Pedo wrote:
They witness daylight, but the sun is left

01:12:16.390 --> 01:12:17.390
far behind.

01:12:17.390 --> 01:12:21.230
They are long exiled from the well-known limits
of the world.

01:12:21.230 --> 01:12:26.120
They have dared to go into the forbidden gloom-filled
territories at the edge of creation to try

01:12:26.120 --> 01:12:29.100
and reach its farthest shores.

01:12:29.100 --> 01:12:35.690
Now they behold the Ocean, which has monsters
beneath its sluggish waves.

01:12:35.690 --> 01:12:39.010
On all sides are savage sharks and dogs of
the sea.

01:12:39.010 --> 01:12:44.660
Crashing waves and wrathful billowing winds
seize their ships, rising them high in the

01:12:44.660 --> 01:12:47.630
water and swelling them with fear.

01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:53.410
As their fleet is caught by swift winds they
feel their ships impacting on a shoal.

01:12:53.410 --> 01:12:59.030
The remorseless fates will mangle them or
deliver them, to the wild creatures of the

01:12:59.030 --> 01:13:00.690
ocean.

01:13:00.690 --> 01:13:03.670
One man stands defiant on the high prow.

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:09.890
He cries out the thoughts of his imprisoned
soul: 'Where are we being taken by the storm?

01:13:09.890 --> 01:13:14.500
Should we even be searching for nations who
dwell beyond another sky?

01:13:14.500 --> 01:13:19.230
We are violating foreign seas and sacred waters
with our oars.

01:13:19.230 --> 01:13:25.480
Why do we trouble the untouched dwelling-places
of the gods?’

01:13:25.480 --> 01:13:29.770
During the storm, the great fleet was smashed
apart and dispersed with an enormous loss

01:13:29.770 --> 01:13:30.870
of life, equipment, and prestige.

01:13:30.870 --> 01:13:36.040
In the aftermath of the disaster, Tiberius
finally abandoned Roman plans to recover the

01:13:36.040 --> 01:13:47.670
region and Greater Germania remained permanently
outside the Empire.

01:13:47.670 --> 01:13:52.810
Due to this decision, Baltic territories beyond
the Jutland Peninsula remained poorly explored

01:13:52.810 --> 01:13:54.620
by Roman authorities.

01:13:54.620 --> 01:14:00.160
In his Natural History completed in AD 75,
Pliny the Elder still relies on the accounts

01:14:00.160 --> 01:14:03.240
of Pytheas to describe the territories beyond
Jutland.

01:14:03.240 --> 01:14:06.230
He writes:
Pytheas says that a German people called the

01:14:06.230 --> 01:14:10.920
Gutones inhabit the shores of an estuary of
the Ocean called Mentonomon.

01:14:10.920 --> 01:14:14.390
Their territory extends six thousand stadia
(600 miles).

01:14:14.390 --> 01:14:18.510
The islanders burn the amber as fuel or trade
it with their neighbours’.

01:14:18.510 --> 01:14:24.400
The ‘Gutones’ are possibly ancestors of
the Goths, who centuries later would invade

01:14:24.400 --> 01:14:29.409
the Roman Empire contributing to its decline,
fragmentation and eventual collapse.

01:14:31.352 --> 01:14:41.697
North, South, East and West - had they yet
reached the ends of the earth?

01:14:55.100 --> 01:14:57.750
The Romans had finally broken through.

01:14:57.750 --> 01:15:02.330
They had completed a sudden and relentless
march through the mountains on the west flank

01:15:02.330 --> 01:15:03.820
of Britain.

01:15:03.820 --> 01:15:08.280
Finally reaching the uncharted west coast,
they now stood on the very shores of the Irish

01:15:08.280 --> 01:15:09.600
Sea.

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:14.700
Within their sight lay the island of Mona
– a sacred druidic centre and powerful base

01:15:14.700 --> 01:15:18.770
for Celtic opposition to Rome.

01:15:18.770 --> 01:15:23.600
This force was commanded by the Governor Suetonius
Paulinus, the same man who had crossed the

01:15:23.600 --> 01:15:27.230
High Atlas Mountains in North Africa two decades
earlier.

01:15:27.230 --> 01:15:32.500
Now he found himself in the mountainous landscape
of Wales receiving the final details of the

01:15:32.500 --> 01:15:34.610
coming attack against the Celtic resistance.

01:15:34.610 --> 01:15:39.579
He had summoned his senior military staff
to the campaign tent to explain their orders

01:15:39.579 --> 01:15:44.620
- and among them was a young twenty-year old
Tribune named Gnaeus Julius Agricola.

01:15:44.620 --> 01:15:52.909
The druids and the leaders of the Celtic resistance
had considered themselves invulnerable within

01:15:52.909 --> 01:15:54.980
their island stronghold of Mona.

01:15:54.980 --> 01:15:58.270
But Paulinus had a plan.

01:15:58.270 --> 01:16:03.750
Under the orders of Paulinus the Classis Britannica
had constructed special flat-bottom landing

01:16:03.750 --> 01:16:04.750
craft.

01:16:04.750 --> 01:16:09.040
The fleet would convey the assembled legions
across the narrow Menai Strait to assault

01:16:09.040 --> 01:16:10.750
the sacred island.

01:16:10.750 --> 01:16:17.020
But as the Romans prepared to disembark on
the shores of Mona, a hideous sight was revealed.

01:16:17.020 --> 01:16:21.889
For the druids had prepared an army of Celtic
tribes.

01:16:21.889 --> 01:16:27.449
A fanatical enemy was invoking terrible curses
on the incoming legionaries.

01:16:27.449 --> 01:16:33.040
Black-clad priestess carrying flaming torches
ran between the ranks of frenzied warriors

01:16:33.040 --> 01:16:34.900
shrieking like crows.

01:16:34.900 --> 01:16:38.130
Fearful paralysis gripped the Romans.

01:16:38.130 --> 01:16:43.900
Perhaps there were indeed mystic powers and
evil entities on the fringes of the known

01:16:43.900 --> 01:16:46.390
world?

01:16:46.390 --> 01:16:55.790
For a long time, the Greeks and Romans had
feared and respected the ancient Celts.

01:16:55.790 --> 01:17:00.620
People from classical civilisations recognised
common images and motifs amongst the cattle

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:02.840
raising cultures of northwest Europe.

01:17:02.840 --> 01:17:07.110
Indeed, early Greek myths of Hercules may
have been influenced by the Celtic gods as

01:17:07.110 --> 01:17:09.810
they appeared on early artifacts or religious
items.

01:17:09.810 --> 01:17:16.750
Lugh of the Longarm was a radiant Celtic warrior
god who encouraged cattle raids - master of

01:17:16.750 --> 01:17:21.239
the spear and slingshot, adept at oratory
and unsurpassed in all the artisan skills

01:17:21.239 --> 01:17:26.860
of craftsmanship - he was represented with
three heads symbolising the three great aspects

01:17:26.860 --> 01:17:28.860
of his supernatural character.

01:17:28.860 --> 01:17:33.949
The Corleck Head from ancient Ireland depicts
the deity with three separate faces on a single

01:17:33.949 --> 01:17:35.130
stone cranium.

01:17:35.130 --> 01:17:40.469
In early Greek myths, the tenth great labour
of Hercules was to enter the distant Atlantic

01:17:40.469 --> 01:17:43.949
and steal the red cattle of the monstrous
giant Geryon.

01:17:43.949 --> 01:17:50.840
Geryon was a triple headed entity with a fearsome
hound that guarded his island home in the

01:17:50.840 --> 01:17:52.940
far west.

01:17:52.940 --> 01:17:58.720
Perhaps the Romans were right to fear Celtic
rituals - Julius Caesar had heard reports

01:17:58.720 --> 01:18:04.100
that the continental Celts burnt sacrificial
victims in giant wicker structures whilst

01:18:04.100 --> 01:18:10.489
leading two expeditions into southern Britain
in 55 and 54 BC, suppressing tribes who had

01:18:10.489 --> 01:18:12.960
supported the Gauls against Rome.

01:18:12.960 --> 01:18:18.139
The Celtic islands had other strange sights
and wonders such as extraordinary war-horns,

01:18:18.139 --> 01:18:19.980
known as caryxns.

01:18:19.980 --> 01:18:25.460
These tall instruments were shaped like gaping
mouths of hideous creatures and some had hinged

01:18:25.460 --> 01:18:28.130
tongues that clacked as the warriors advanced.

01:18:28.130 --> 01:18:34.230
In a Celtic army the trumpet-like horns projected
far above the heads of the tallest warriors,

01:18:34.230 --> 01:18:39.350
so that the monstrous howling creatures seemed
to join and jostle for place in the frenzied

01:18:39.350 --> 01:18:41.770
battle line.

01:18:41.770 --> 01:18:46.340
And so in preparation for combat with these
distant horrors the Emperor Claudius brought

01:18:46.340 --> 01:18:53.720
his own monsters to Britain when he invaded
the island in AD 43.

01:18:53.720 --> 01:18:55.900
War-elephants.

01:18:55.900 --> 01:19:00.660
The Celts venerated bristle-backed wild boars
as powerful warrior totems.

01:19:00.660 --> 01:19:07.020
So massive elephants, with their long tusks
and trumpeting roar, were truly horrifying.

01:19:07.020 --> 01:19:12.400
Claudius´ victory in Britain was seen as
a triumph over the great northern ocean itself.

01:19:12.400 --> 01:19:15.800
Suetonius describes the ceremonies in Rome
where:

01:19:15.800 --> 01:19:20.850
Among the spoils of victory, Claudius fixed
a naval crown next to the civic crown on the

01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:23.219
pediment of the Palatine Palace.

01:19:23.219 --> 01:19:32.909
This was a sign that he had crossed over and
conquered the Ocean.

01:19:32.909 --> 01:19:37.620
Suetonius Paulinus, the Roman general who
crossed the High Atlas Mountains, became the

01:19:37.620 --> 01:19:39.850
governor of Britannia in AD 58.

01:19:39.850 --> 01:19:45.449
The small offshore province of Britain was
well-established, but most of the island remained

01:19:45.449 --> 01:19:47.460
beyond Roman control.

01:19:47.460 --> 01:19:52.080
Paulinus was given the position by Nero, but
he still maintained his own personal motives

01:19:52.080 --> 01:19:53.080
and interests.

01:19:53.080 --> 01:19:58.800
In particular, he was keen to outdo the exploits
of a contemporary named Corbulo who was conducting

01:19:58.800 --> 01:20:02.660
a dangerous war against the Parthians in mountainous
Armenia.

01:20:02.660 --> 01:20:09.350
Compelled by this rivalry, Paulinus led his
own military expedition into unknown hostile

01:20:09.350 --> 01:20:11.410
British territories.

01:20:11.410 --> 01:20:15.570
Perhaps it was Nero who encouraged renewed
action on the frontiers, or maybe Paulinus

01:20:15.570 --> 01:20:18.395
was inspired by current events - indeed in
the east Corbulo was campaigning in Armenia,

01:20:18.395 --> 01:20:23.110
in the south Praetorians were exploring the
Nile and an equites had embarked north to

01:20:23.110 --> 01:20:24.880
find the distant source of amber.

01:20:24.880 --> 01:20:28.790
Could Paulinus not do likewise?

01:20:28.790 --> 01:20:33.469
He could lead his legions to the western edge
of Britain and look across the Irish Sea at

01:20:33.469 --> 01:20:35.910
the furthest unconquered lands.

01:20:35.910 --> 01:20:41.500
How could Nero with his fascination with exploration
and distant lands possibly object?

01:20:41.500 --> 01:20:45.610
And so, Paulinus had marched his legions westward.

01:20:45.610 --> 01:20:49.320
Tacitus reports:
Now, Britain was under the command of Suetonius

01:20:49.320 --> 01:20:55.350
Paulinus, a man who vied with Corbulo in attaining
military experience and popular favour.

01:20:55.350 --> 01:20:59.980
Paulinus therefore prepared to attack the
island of Mona which had a powerful population

01:20:59.980 --> 01:21:02.480
and was a refuge for fugitives.

01:21:02.480 --> 01:21:07.860
On the opposite shore stood the opposing army
with a dense array of well-armed warriors.

01:21:07.860 --> 01:21:12.179
Between their ranks dashed women with their
hair dishevelled, dressed in black attire

01:21:12.179 --> 01:21:14.520
like the Furies and waving firebrands.

01:21:14.520 --> 01:21:21.200
All around, were the Druids, lifting their
hands up to heaven, and pouring forth dreadful

01:21:21.200 --> 01:21:22.340
incantations.

01:21:22.340 --> 01:21:28.470
Our soldiers were terrified by the unfamiliar
sight and paused as if their limbs were paralysed.

01:21:28.470 --> 01:21:34.340
They stood motionless and exposed to wounds.

01:21:34.340 --> 01:21:38.410
Suetonius yelled the orders.

01:21:38.410 --> 01:21:39.410
‘Equaliter Ambula – Percute!’

01:21:39.410 --> 01:21:41.730
– ‘Advance in Order – Charge!’.

01:21:41.730 --> 01:21:46.660
The spell was broken and the ranks advanced.

01:21:46.660 --> 01:21:49.040
It was soon over.

01:21:49.040 --> 01:21:54.120
The well organised soldiers butchered the
enemy and burnt their sacred grooves.

01:21:54.120 --> 01:21:58.660
Another enemy of Rome had been destroyed.

01:21:58.660 --> 01:22:02.850
The young Agricola participated in the military
actions, learning the skills and attitudes

01:22:02.850 --> 01:22:06.760
that would one day inform and guide his own
decisions in high office.

01:22:06.760 --> 01:22:13.400
But as the Romans dismantled the Celtic shrines,
news suddenly reached Paulinus that the province

01:22:13.400 --> 01:22:16.100
of Britain was in revolt.

01:22:16.100 --> 01:22:21.120
On the east coast, the previously submissive
Iceni tribe had rebelled.

01:22:21.120 --> 01:22:26.040
Their queen Boudicia re-armed her people and
inflicted devastation on the poorly defended

01:22:26.040 --> 01:22:27.429
Roman communities.

01:22:27.429 --> 01:22:32.370
Suetonius led a rushed and desperate march
back across the mountains to rescue the province.

01:22:32.370 --> 01:22:38.250
Though the outnumbered Romans were victorious
in battle, the damage had been done.

01:22:38.250 --> 01:22:42.909
Entire populations were butchered or fled
as refugees as the new colonies and cities

01:22:42.909 --> 01:22:45.280
were burnt or abandoned.

01:22:45.280 --> 01:22:50.400
Nero considered withdrawing the legions permanently
from Britain, but for the sake of Honour,

01:22:50.400 --> 01:22:52.810
the province was retained.

01:22:52.810 --> 01:22:58.420
Nevertheless, lessons were learned, and further
expansion halted for more than a decade.

01:22:58.420 --> 01:23:04.179
Writing in AD 75 Pliny claims that Britain
has not been explored beyond the Caledonian

01:23:04.179 --> 01:23:07.760
Forest (ancient Scotland).

01:23:07.760 --> 01:23:15.310
And so, in the far north, at the crest of
the world - what lay beyond?

01:23:15.310 --> 01:23:21.771
And who would dare make this journey?

01:23:35.790 --> 01:23:40.600
During the political highpoint of the Roman
Empire, two distant travellers met by chance

01:23:40.600 --> 01:23:44.070
at the sacred site of Delphi in central Greece.

01:23:44.070 --> 01:23:49.190
They began discussing the extent and form
of the world as revealed by ancient texts

01:23:49.190 --> 01:23:54.080
and their own explorations in far away lands
on the edge of the empire.

01:23:54.080 --> 01:23:58.840
An account of their conversation was recorded
by the Greek writer Plutarch, who says his

01:23:58.840 --> 01:24:02.900
brother Lamprias joined the travellers in
their discussions.

01:24:02.900 --> 01:24:08.370
Delphi was considered to be the sacred cultural
centre of Greece and the midpoint of the entire

01:24:08.370 --> 01:24:09.810
Ancient World.

01:24:09.810 --> 01:24:13.930
It was known as the omphalos, or naval of
the earth.

01:24:13.930 --> 01:24:18.820
A myth described how Zeus had simultaneously
launched two eagles from the opposite edges

01:24:18.820 --> 01:24:23.310
of the globe, and the birds met one another
in the skies directly above Delphi.

01:24:23.310 --> 01:24:29.989
The two travellers were Cleombrotus of Sparta
and Demetrius of Tarsus, and like the eagles

01:24:29.989 --> 01:24:35.320
of Zeus, each had returned from opposite edges
of the known world.

01:24:35.320 --> 01:24:41.100
Cleombrotus was a wealthy traveller from the
ancient territory of Sparta in southern Greece.

01:24:41.100 --> 01:24:45.730
He had journeyed for knowledge and experience
across the eastern ocean to India.

01:24:45.730 --> 01:24:50.200
Plutarch writes:
The journeys by Cleombrotus were not for business,

01:24:50.200 --> 01:24:53.860
he was fond of seeing things and of acquiring
knowledge.

01:24:53.860 --> 01:24:58.360
He had sufficient wealth and felt that it
was not any great advantage to have more than

01:24:58.360 --> 01:24:59.360
enough.

01:24:59.360 --> 01:25:03.040
So he spent his leisure time involved in travelling
and enquiry.

01:25:03.040 --> 01:25:06.120
But Demetrius was different.

01:25:06.120 --> 01:25:10.190
He had been on a very different mission.

01:25:10.190 --> 01:25:15.980
He was a grammarian, a language expert, who
had been recruited as a military agent and

01:25:15.980 --> 01:25:20.659
had been on an imperial assignment to the
mysterious Celtic islands off the northwest

01:25:20.659 --> 01:25:22.500
coast of Britain.

01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:26.800
Plutarch writes:
Demetrius said that there were many isolated

01:25:26.800 --> 01:25:29.370
islands positioned near Britain.

01:25:29.370 --> 01:25:35.460
These islands had few or no inhabitants and
some bore the names of divinities or heroes.

01:25:35.460 --> 01:25:41.330
He himself, by the emperor's order, had made
a voyage for inquiry and observation to the

01:25:41.330 --> 01:25:45.330
nearest of these islands.

01:25:45.330 --> 01:25:51.980
Agricola, the Governor of Roman Britain, had
requested Greek intellectuals for some great

01:25:51.980 --> 01:25:54.960
venture in the north Atlantic.

01:25:54.960 --> 01:25:58.500
Demetrius had answered this call.

01:25:58.500 --> 01:26:03.690
In his role as imperial agent, he had been
sent to isolated islands off the coast of

01:26:03.690 --> 01:26:08.460
ancient Britain - probably travelling aboard
a Roman war-galley, one of many that had been

01:26:08.460 --> 01:26:13.659
sent to explore and map a series of islands
and coastlines located to the west of Britain

01:26:13.659 --> 01:26:14.659
and the north of Ireland.

01:26:14.659 --> 01:26:21.330
The sudden approach of an Atlantic storm drove
the ship and its crew to seek shelter ashore.

01:26:21.330 --> 01:26:27.560
The Roman explorers were forced to take refuge
on some strange island for several days.

01:26:27.560 --> 01:26:32.001
Intrigued by this edge of the world territory,
Demetrius managed to communicate with the

01:26:32.001 --> 01:26:38.170
inhabitants in their own dialect - and was
told that they were trespassing on a holy

01:26:38.170 --> 01:26:39.380
island.

01:26:39.380 --> 01:26:45.900
Somewhere nearby was resting place of a dormant
god that Demetrius identified as Kronos.

01:26:45.900 --> 01:26:51.580
The Greek god Kronos was the primordial being
overthrown by Zeus.

01:26:51.580 --> 01:26:56.850
Demetrius would have also heard stories about
the vast ceremonial mounds of prehistoric

01:26:56.850 --> 01:27:03.460
peoples who lived thousands of years before
the Celts and mysterious stone circles constructed

01:27:03.460 --> 01:27:06.340
by Neolithic populations.

01:27:06.340 --> 01:27:10.480
Roman galleys charting the north coast of
Ireland would have already seen the giant

01:27:10.480 --> 01:27:15.619
paved causeway extending from the rugged coast
into the depths of the sea.

01:27:15.619 --> 01:27:21.770
This undersea causeway rose again on a dark
island where a vast multi-columned cave emitted

01:27:21.770 --> 01:27:24.660
strange acoustic thrumings.

01:27:24.660 --> 01:27:30.390
This unique natural feature on the Isle of
Staffa is known in modern times as Fingal’s

01:27:30.390 --> 01:27:31.390
Cave.

01:27:31.390 --> 01:27:36.100
The close-fitting hexagonal stones of the
Giant’s Causeway and Fingal’s Cave must

01:27:36.100 --> 01:27:40.460
have been viewed as the work of deities or
great heroes.

01:27:40.460 --> 01:27:47.770
It was clear the Roman fleet was trespassing
on the outer world of the gods.

01:27:47.770 --> 01:27:55.300
Other questions had to be solved if the Romans
hoped to master this new northern environment.

01:27:55.300 --> 01:28:00.290
Was Britain an island of unparalleled size
or simply the outer reaches of some great

01:28:00.290 --> 01:28:04.940
northern counter-land that extended deep into
the hostile Arctic Zone?

01:28:04.940 --> 01:28:09.810
Did this landmass have a terminus?

01:28:09.810 --> 01:28:14.190
Even half away across the world, Britain was
famous for its mysterious size.

01:28:14.190 --> 01:28:19.360
In AD 66 the Jewish king Herod Agrippa had
made a speech to his people on the eve of

01:28:19.360 --> 01:28:20.900
a failed revolt.

01:28:20.900 --> 01:28:26.409
Josephus reports:
Do not depend on the walls of Jerusalem . . . The

01:28:26.409 --> 01:28:29.680
Romans have sought another habitable earth
beyond the ocean.

01:28:29.680 --> 01:28:33.989
They have landed armies on British islands
which were never known before.

01:28:33.989 --> 01:28:36.010
Consider what a barrier the Britons had.

01:28:36.010 --> 01:28:40.930
They were encircled by the Ocean and yet the
Romans sailed across and subdued them.

01:28:40.930 --> 01:28:46.350
And the Britons inhabit a landmass that may
not be smaller than this entire inhabited

01:28:46.350 --> 01:28:48.220
earth.

01:28:48.220 --> 01:28:54.400
And so in AD 77, On the orders of the Emperor
Vespasian, the newly appointed governor Gnaeus

01:28:54.400 --> 01:29:00.469
Julius Agricola was instructed to find and
secure these frontiers wherever they might

01:29:00.469 --> 01:29:03.820
turn out to be.

01:29:03.820 --> 01:29:12.980
Agricola led Roman forces to the edge of the
Highlands and began establishing the Firth-Clyde

01:29:12.980 --> 01:29:14.390
Frontier.

01:29:14.390 --> 01:29:19.480
This was a forty-mile-wide defensive network
of forts and embankments that would form a

01:29:19.480 --> 01:29:23.850
permanent northern limit to the British province.

01:29:23.850 --> 01:29:30.560
Now a new conquest could begin so that ‘freedom
would be removed from sight’ and the Caledonians

01:29:30.560 --> 01:29:33.440
would be entirely isolated.

01:29:33.440 --> 01:29:38.860
And in AD 80 the Romans prepared their fleet
for the conquest and annexation of Ireland

01:29:38.860 --> 01:29:42.290
- the final great refuge of the Celtic peoples.

01:29:42.290 --> 01:29:47.540
This was to be the Empire’s final conquest
and would complete their exploration of the

01:29:47.540 --> 01:29:48.820
distant west.

01:29:48.820 --> 01:29:53.080
Tacitus reports:
‘In soil and climate, in the disposition

01:29:53.080 --> 01:29:57.540
and lifestyle of its population, Ireland differs
little from Britain.

01:29:57.540 --> 01:30:01.790
The routes of approach and harbours are better
known to us through commerce and merchants

01:30:01.790 --> 01:30:08.159
. . . it would unite the most powerful parts
of the Empire with great mutual benefit.

01:30:08.159 --> 01:30:15.760
But unforeseen events coincided to halt the
Roman invasion of Ireland.

01:30:15.760 --> 01:30:24.040
In AD 81 the Emperor Titus, the eldest son
of Vespasian, suddenly died after only a year

01:30:24.040 --> 01:30:25.040
in office.

01:30:25.040 --> 01:30:29.290
He was succeeded by his younger brother Domitian
who had different plans for Empire.

01:30:29.290 --> 01:30:35.440
And at almost the same time, a remarkable
account was circulating.

01:30:35.440 --> 01:30:38.120
It was the story of a voyage.

01:30:38.120 --> 01:30:44.560
A newly recruited cohort of Germanic auxiliaries
had mutinied while stationed on the west coast

01:30:44.560 --> 01:30:45.560
of Britain.

01:30:45.560 --> 01:30:50.000
They had murdered their commanding officers
and seized three war-galleys to embark on

01:30:50.000 --> 01:30:51.000
their escape.

01:30:51.000 --> 01:30:56.010
Their aim was to search for some sea route
first north, then eastward.

01:30:56.010 --> 01:30:59.040
back to their homelands beyond the Rhine.

01:30:59.040 --> 01:31:03.969
The emaciated survivors had reached northern
Germany on a single remaining ship, but they

01:31:03.969 --> 01:31:09.790
had been swiftly recaptured and returned to
Roman authorities on the Rhine Frontier.

01:31:09.790 --> 01:31:15.600
The survivors had told a remarkable story
of great daring and hardships, ferocious storms,

01:31:15.600 --> 01:31:17.340
sea raids and cannibalism.

01:31:17.340 --> 01:31:23.560
They were the living proof that some sort
of sea route existed around the northern coasts

01:31:23.560 --> 01:31:24.560
of Britain.

01:31:24.560 --> 01:31:31.449
Britain was confirmed to be an island - with
its furthest limits just beyond the contested

01:31:31.449 --> 01:31:33.600
highland zone.

01:31:33.600 --> 01:31:39.070
By coincidence rumours now reached Agricola
of a major uprising in the Highlands.

01:31:39.070 --> 01:31:43.940
In response Agricola called off the Irish
campaign and turned his full military force

01:31:43.940 --> 01:31:48.010
northward against this encroaching force of
Highland Celts.

01:31:48.010 --> 01:31:53.030
The Roman fleet joined with the land forces
to pursue the enemy into hostile territory,

01:31:53.030 --> 01:31:58.160
entering lochs and deep ocean inlets on the
western coast of Scotland.

01:31:58.160 --> 01:32:06.530
Tacitus reports:
Nowhere does the sea hold wider influence

01:32:06.530 --> 01:32:07.750
over land.

01:32:07.750 --> 01:32:10.270
The tidal currents flow this way and that.

01:32:10.270 --> 01:32:15.740
For the sea does not merely ebb and flow along
the coast, but it penetrates and winds its

01:32:15.740 --> 01:32:21.679
way deep inland, placing itself amidst the
ridges and mountains and forming its own domain

01:32:21.679 --> 01:32:24.659
[…]
For the first time the fleet was employed

01:32:24.659 --> 01:32:28.949
as an integral part of the land army and ships
accompanied the campaign.

01:32:28.949 --> 01:32:34.159
The imposing façade of war was pushed forward
both by sea and land.

01:32:34.159 --> 01:32:39.370
Often Roman infantry, cavalry, and marines,
shared the same encampment, cheerfully taking

01:32:39.370 --> 01:32:40.970
the same meals.

01:32:40.970 --> 01:32:45.610
They would evaluate battles on land with victories
on the Ocean.

01:32:45.610 --> 01:32:50.590
Two years of difficult campaigning followed
before the Romans were able to inflict a serious

01:32:50.590 --> 01:32:52.810
defeat on their elusive enemy.

01:32:52.810 --> 01:32:58.270
Late in the summer of AD 83 Agricola led his
legions deep into the Highland wilderness

01:32:58.270 --> 01:33:03.390
to attack a major gathering of Caledonian
warriors at a site called Mons Graupius.

01:33:03.390 --> 01:33:09.790
With this victory, Agricola could claim that
Caledonia was finally subdued.

01:33:09.790 --> 01:33:15.770
And so he marched north with the legions to
find the bleak limits of the land.

01:33:15.770 --> 01:33:20.250
Under his orders the fleet based in the Clyde
estuary conducted its voyage of exploration

01:33:20.250 --> 01:33:25.340
northward to confirm the outer limits of the
British landmass by sea.

01:33:25.340 --> 01:33:32.210
And there, as winter was approaching, they
observed another island in the far north,

01:33:32.210 --> 01:33:35.210
beyond Britain.

01:33:35.210 --> 01:33:39.770
Surely the land encountered beyond these shores
must be the semi-mythical Thule?

01:33:39.770 --> 01:33:42.600
Perhaps bad weather prevented the fleet commander
from ordering a landing and organising a shore

01:33:42.600 --> 01:33:43.600
party.

01:33:43.600 --> 01:33:44.600
Or maybe he was reserving this honour for
Agrippa himself.

01:33:44.600 --> 01:33:45.900
Tacitus reports:
There is a vast irregular tract of land stretching

01:33:45.900 --> 01:33:50.850
out from the furthermost shore of Caledonia
and tapering to a kind of wedge.

01:33:50.850 --> 01:33:55.770
This was the first time that a Roman fleet
had circumnavigated this coast and crossed

01:33:55.770 --> 01:33:57.210
the remotest sea.

01:33:57.210 --> 01:33:59.400
They confirmed that Britain was indeed an
island.

01:33:59.400 --> 01:34:00.750
They also discovered and conquered a series
of previously unknown islands called the Orcades.

01:34:00.750 --> 01:34:05.160
Although they sighted Thule, no landing was
made, since no orders for this purpose had

01:34:05.160 --> 01:34:06.761
been issued and winter was approaching.

01:34:06.761 --> 01:34:07.761
The fleet reported that the sea in this region
was sluggish and heavy to the oars.

01:34:07.761 --> 01:34:08.761
They concluded that this was because deep
waters are not easily set in motion by the

01:34:08.761 --> 01:34:09.761
winds as they are in other seas.

01:34:09.761 --> 01:34:10.761
The land and mountains, which are the cause
of storms, are distant from this sea.

01:34:10.761 --> 01:34:11.761
So, the deep mass of the open ocean has a
far slower momentum.

01:34:11.761 --> 01:34:12.761
In the next campaign season the Romans might
have followed this chain of outer islands

01:34:12.761 --> 01:34:16.890
northward towards Iceland and the edge of
the Arctic Circle.

01:34:16.890 --> 01:34:20.239
But Agricola never undertook this venture.

01:34:20.239 --> 01:34:25.679
He was recalled from Britain and the fleet
never re-entered these remote seas.

01:34:25.679 --> 01:34:30.060
Further expansion had become impractical when
the Emperor Domitian recalled essential troops

01:34:30.060 --> 01:34:36.490
from Britain - urgently needed to defend the
threatened Danube frontier and protect long-established

01:34:36.490 --> 01:34:38.909
imperial possessions.

01:34:38.909 --> 01:34:44.790
Rome was on the defensive and it was the embattled
legions who watched the frontiers with fear

01:34:44.790 --> 01:34:46.480
and apprehension.

01:34:46.480 --> 01:34:51.850
The captured territories in Caledonia were
abandoned and Ireland was never added to the

01:34:51.850 --> 01:34:54.400
empire.

01:34:54.400 --> 01:34:59.210
Standing on a wet promontory above the Roman
fleet, after the battle of Mons Graupius,

01:34:59.210 --> 01:35:05.340
Gnaeus Julius Agricola had indeed stood at
the maritime edge of the habitable world - and

01:35:05.340 --> 01:35:12.043
it would remain that way for centuries afterwards.

01:35:25.950 --> 01:35:28.330
It is the second century AD.

01:35:28.330 --> 01:35:32.440
Roman civilisation has reached its greatest
achievements.

01:35:32.440 --> 01:35:38.350
Alexandria, the capital of Egypt, stands in
the centre of a vast world system that connects

01:35:38.350 --> 01:35:42.430
the most distant regions through vibrant commercial
networks.

01:35:42.430 --> 01:35:48.409
Hundreds of ships deliver thousands of tons
of Egyptian grain to Rome to feed a massive

01:35:48.409 --> 01:35:49.409
consumer population.

01:35:49.409 --> 01:35:54.030
But Alexandria also looks eastward, and its
merchants could reach southern India in just

01:35:54.030 --> 01:35:55.620
seventy days.

01:35:55.620 --> 01:36:01.199
The Emperor Trajan has restored an ancient
canal linking the Nile to the Suez Gulf.

01:36:01.199 --> 01:36:05.460
This allows travellers to avoid the hardships
of the Eastern Desert on their journeys to

01:36:05.460 --> 01:36:06.889
the Red Sea ports.

01:36:06.889 --> 01:36:13.670
Italy and India are connected by trade through
the great metropolis of Alexandria.

01:36:13.670 --> 01:36:20.060
In the great Library of Alexandria, Claudius
Ptolemy is redrawing and expanding the world-map

01:36:20.060 --> 01:36:24.409
devised by his predecessor Marinos of Tyre.

01:36:24.409 --> 01:36:29.880
Roman political and economic power has greatly
expanded classical geography and its merchants

01:36:29.880 --> 01:36:34.869
repeatedly pass what used to be the boundaries
of the known world.

01:36:34.869 --> 01:36:40.380
Unfortunately he has miscalculated the size
of the earth - reducing it to about 72% of

01:36:40.380 --> 01:36:45.430
actual size - his landmass measurements are
too large on this reduced globe and northern

01:36:45.430 --> 01:36:49.340
Britain extends too far into the Arctic Zone.

01:36:49.340 --> 01:36:54.800
Ptolemy therefore ‘adjusts’ Caledonia,
by making the northern part of Britain bend

01:36:54.800 --> 01:36:56.630
west-east.

01:36:56.630 --> 01:37:01.070
This is a minor change to a territory that
was no longer part of the Empire, but one

01:37:01.070 --> 01:37:06.000
consequence was that Shetland is pushed eastward
towards Scandinavia.

01:37:06.000 --> 01:37:10.520
But what about Thule?

01:37:10.520 --> 01:37:13.280
Ptolemy carefully reviews the map data collected
by Agricola.

01:37:13.280 --> 01:37:19.250
By all accounts Thule should be north of Britain,
so he therefore plots the coordinates of Shetland

01:37:19.250 --> 01:37:24.550
on his world map and labels this landmass
‘Thule’.

01:37:24.550 --> 01:37:38.460
The Romans had found and mapped their Thule
- and the credit went to Agricola.

01:37:38.460 --> 01:37:42.310
Advances in geographical knowledge were also
occurring on the eastern edges of the known

01:37:42.310 --> 01:37:43.310
world.

01:37:43.310 --> 01:37:48.369
A merchant sailor named Alexandros wrote a
new Periplus that outlined Roman voyages across

01:37:48.369 --> 01:37:50.600
the eastern Indian Ocean.

01:37:50.600 --> 01:37:56.230
Roman merchant ships now reached distant kingdoms
such as Burma and by following Indian sailing

01:37:56.230 --> 01:38:02.020
routes around the Malay Peninsula, they arrived
at the previously remote islands of Sumatra,

01:38:02.020 --> 01:38:04.139
Java, and Borneo.

01:38:04.139 --> 01:38:09.679
Roman sailing records suggest that beast-like
satyrs inhabited these islands.

01:38:09.679 --> 01:38:10.679
These could be orangutans.

01:38:10.679 --> 01:38:11.995
Ancient Buddhist scriptures describe the early
settlement of these jungle-covered territories

01:38:11.995 --> 01:38:12.995
in Indonesia and beyond.

01:38:12.995 --> 01:38:17.659
The Romans called this distant sea the ‘Bay
of Sinai’ because some unknown route led

01:38:17.659 --> 01:38:20.110
north to the outer territories of Han China.

01:38:20.110 --> 01:38:27.480
The furthest location on these distant voyages
was a mysterious trade outpost known as Kattigara.

01:38:27.480 --> 01:38:31.409
Ptolemy records :
Marinos does not provide a distance in stades

01:38:31.409 --> 01:38:34.280
for the sail from the Golden Peninsula to
Kattigara.

01:38:34.280 --> 01:38:40.360
However, he does record that Alexandros had
written that the land from there curves south.

01:38:40.360 --> 01:38:45.600
Those who sail along it reach the city of
Zabai in twenty days and after sailing from

01:38:45.600 --> 01:38:50.099
Zabai northeast for few days they reach Kattigara.

01:38:50.099 --> 01:38:54.170
Perhaps Kattigara was an oriental city somewhere
on the coast of Vietnam.

01:38:54.170 --> 01:38:59.290
It could have been visited by Chinese merchants
from the nearby Han Empire which the Romans

01:38:59.290 --> 01:39:01.610
knew as Sinae.

01:39:01.610 --> 01:39:06.850
But Roman merchants were also extending classical
knowledge as they travelled eastwards along

01:39:06.850 --> 01:39:12.349
the more direct land-based Silk Routes to
China.

01:39:12.349 --> 01:39:16.969
In about AD 100, merchants were sent east
by an influential Roman businessman named

01:39:16.969 --> 01:39:18.720
Maes Titianus.

01:39:18.720 --> 01:39:23.210
They were granted passage through the rival
Parthian Empire which occupied Iran.

01:39:23.210 --> 01:39:28.660
Trekking through Bactria – ancient Afghanistan
– on trade routes across the powerful Kushan

01:39:28.660 --> 01:39:34.100
Empire, their destination was a trade outpost
close to the distant Pamir Mountains, known

01:39:34.100 --> 01:39:38.310
to merchants as ‘The Stone Tower’.

01:39:38.310 --> 01:39:43.849
Here caravans coming from Indian and Iranian
territories met with Tarim merchants and steppe

01:39:43.849 --> 01:39:45.119
traders.

01:39:45.119 --> 01:39:52.880
Bullion and rare substances were exchanged
at the Stone Tower for precious Chinese silk.

01:39:52.880 --> 01:39:57.540
The Roman merchants who reached the Stone
Tower in AD 100 seized the opportunity to

01:39:57.540 --> 01:40:04.040
travel onwards into the homelands of the mysterious
‘Seres’ – the ancient ‘Silk People’.

01:40:04.040 --> 01:40:08.290
Ptolemy records:
The route from the Stone Tower to the Seres

01:40:08.290 --> 01:40:13.690
is subject to extreme weather, so there must
have been numerous pauses in the journey.

01:40:13.690 --> 01:40:17.310
The route only became known through the opportunities
for commerce.

01:40:17.310 --> 01:40:22.730
Marinos records that one Maes, also known
as Titianus, a Macedonian and a merchant by

01:40:22.730 --> 01:40:25.460
family profession, recorded the distance.

01:40:25.460 --> 01:40:30.830
Maes did not travel the route himself but
sent agents to the Seres [the Chinese].

01:40:30.830 --> 01:40:36.040
And so Ptolemy used the distances provided
by Maes to estimate the vast size of eastern

01:40:36.040 --> 01:40:48.290
Asia, and having settled upon the dimensions
of the land of the Seres, he turned his gaze...southwards.

01:40:48.290 --> 01:40:54.250
By this era Roman merchants, agents, and explorers,
had ventured further into Africa too in pursuit

01:40:54.250 --> 01:40:56.920
of profit, or on special imperial assignments.

01:40:56.920 --> 01:41:00.270
To estimate the southern extent of the known
world, Ptolemy consulted accounts of Roman

01:41:00.270 --> 01:41:04.180
trading voyages to a distant East African
trading station called Rhapta.

01:41:04.180 --> 01:41:12.000
Rhapta was on the coast of Tanzania, near
to the distant and mysterious island of Madagascar.

01:41:12.000 --> 01:41:16.850
Voyages along these coasts included sailings
throughout the night, along shores where the

01:41:16.850 --> 01:41:19.760
coast rose steeply upward to form desolate
cliffs.

01:41:19.760 --> 01:41:20.760
Rhapta was in the Southern Hemisphere, more
than 4,000 miles sailing from Alexandria.

01:41:20.760 --> 01:41:21.760
Details were confirmed by a Greek captain
named Diogenes who encountered a strong northern

01:41:21.760 --> 01:41:22.760
wind near the Horn of Africa and sailed south
to Rhapta in 25 days.

01:41:22.760 --> 01:41:23.760
Diogenes suggested that the true source of
the Nile might lie in this region, as there

01:41:23.760 --> 01:41:24.760
were reports of vast inland lakes – perhaps
Lake Victoria?

01:41:24.760 --> 01:41:25.760
Ptolemy writes:
Marinus records that the sailing between the

01:41:25.760 --> 01:41:29.349
Aromatic Promontory and Rhapta was undertaken
by a certain Diogenes.

01:41:29.349 --> 01:41:33.450
He was returning from India a second time
when he was driven south from the Promontory

01:41:33.450 --> 01:41:35.869
by a strong northern wind.

01:41:35.869 --> 01:41:40.659
He sailed south with Africa on his right for
twenty-five days till he reached a position

01:41:40.659 --> 01:41:48.040
near the lakes from which the Nile flows.

01:41:48.040 --> 01:41:53.980
In the 80s AD, the Emperor Domitian sent an
explorer team across the central Sahara.

01:41:53.980 --> 01:41:59.000
They crossed through Garamatic territory to
enter the Bush Savannas in the lands beyond.

01:41:59.000 --> 01:42:06.190
The explorers entered inner Africa and captured
a rare savannah rhino for display in Rome.

01:42:06.190 --> 01:42:11.110
Ptolemy describes how Julius Meternus spent
four-months on the outward journey to this

01:42:11.110 --> 01:42:12.699
enigmatic land of Agisymba.

01:42:12.699 --> 01:42:15.710
He writes:
Julius Maternus, travelled from Leptis Magna

01:42:15.710 --> 01:42:21.250
to Garamantia and accompanied its king on
an expedition to the Ethiopians.

01:42:21.250 --> 01:42:25.849
After marching south for four months they
reached Agisymba, in the Ethiopian country,

01:42:25.849 --> 01:42:28.010
where the rhinoceros congregate.

01:42:28.010 --> 01:42:33.099
The captive African rhino was transported
a thousand miles across savannah and desert,

01:42:33.099 --> 01:42:36.010
before being placed on a ship bound for Rome.

01:42:36.010 --> 01:42:41.610
There, in the newly built colosseum, Domitian’s
rhino thundered across the arena to impale

01:42:41.610 --> 01:42:44.710
bulls and destroy Caledonian bears.

01:42:44.710 --> 01:42:50.750
To the amazed spectators it was clear that
the emperor Domitian commanded animals that

01:42:50.750 --> 01:42:55.780
were superior to anything Agricola might have
encountered on the northern edge of Europe.

01:42:55.780 --> 01:43:01.820
The Roman crowd realised that the African
continent was extensive and its limits seem

01:43:01.820 --> 01:43:04.750
endless.

01:43:04.750 --> 01:43:11.000
And so, looking at their world maps the Greeks
and Romans had realised that the known earth

01:43:11.000 --> 01:43:14.330
covered only a fraction of the globe.

01:43:14.330 --> 01:43:19.230
Seneca wondered what undiscovered lands might
exist beyond the current reach of Rome.

01:43:19.230 --> 01:43:23.560
He remembered the arrival of the Sinhalese
envoys which permitted distant Sri Lanka to

01:43:23.560 --> 01:43:27.890
become a launching point for Roman voyages
out into a further unknown.

01:43:27.890 --> 01:43:33.980
Would the day come when the Empire could complete
its Atlantic conquests and reach across the

01:43:33.980 --> 01:43:36.990
western ocean to other undiscovered lands?

01:43:36.990 --> 01:43:41.960
He wrote:
There will come an age in future times when

01:43:41.960 --> 01:43:45.330
the Ocean shall unloose the bonds of knowledge.

01:43:45.330 --> 01:43:52.830
When the whole vast earth shall be discovered,
Tethys shall reveal new worlds and Thule will

01:43:52.830 --> 01:43:57.380
not be the limit of the known lands.

01:43:57.380 --> 01:44:03.380
Roman civilisation, through the exploits of
conquest and commerce, had discovered, accessed,

01:44:03.380 --> 01:44:11.400
revealed and mapped the Ancient World as they
knew it - but there was much more to be uncovered.

